/* TIE Tech Tree - ship summaries and per-arrow change notes.
 * Original prose for this site, fact-grounded against Wookieepedia articles. */
window.TIE_LORE = {
 "ships": {
  "starcourier": "The Star Courier was a small, limited-run transport built by Republic Sienar Systems for wealthy diplomats and executives traveling dangerous space. Its layout already anticipated the TIE family: a spherical central cabin, experimental high-temperature ion engines, and large folding radiator panels, defended by laser cannons and exotic sensor systems. In Legends, the class was designed by celebrated shipwright Rugess Nome, secretly the Sith Lord Darth Tenebrous.\n\nIts most famous example was the Scimitar, Darth Maul's Sith Infiltrator, a heavily modified courier carrying six laser cannons, spy and surveillance gear, interrogator droids, and a rare cloaking device. Legends adds that Darth Sidious secretly commissioned it, that a young Raith Sienar delivered it to Darth Plagueis, and that it later passed through Maul, Dooku, and an Imperial Inquisitor.\n\nBoth continuities root the TIE lineage here. The canon TIE Fighter Owners' Workshop Manual links Sienar courier technology to the later fighters, Legends' Essential Guide to Warfare traces the T.I.E. starfighter back to this very courier design, and the canon TIE Advanced v1 was described as heavily based on the Scimitar itself.",
  "eta2": "The Eta-2 Actis-class light interceptor, better known as the Jedi interceptor, was built by Kuat Systems Engineering to succeed the Delta-7 Aethersprite. Only 5.47 meters long, it was flown by Jedi such as Obi-Wan Kenobi, Anakin Skywalker, and Yoda through the final year of the Clone Wars, most famously at the Battle of Coruscant in Revenge of the Sith.\n\nIts design sacrificed everything for performance: no shields, no onboard hyperdrive (an external transport ring was required), thin hull plating, and folding S-foil radiator wings that shed engine heat during hard combat. Armament comprised two laser cannons and two ion cannons, with an externally socketed astromech handling navigation and repairs. It reached 1,500 kph with 5,200-G acceleration.\n\nThe Eta-2 exists in both Canon and Legends. Canonically it is a direct ancestor of the TIE fighter: Raith Sienar drew on its central cockpit pod, twin ion engines, and weapons technology, and Darth Vader kept flying modified Eta-2s well into the Imperial era, a living link between Jedi interceptor and Imperial fighter doctrine.",
  "vwing": "The Alpha-3 Nimbus-class V-wing was a compact interceptor from Kuat Systems Engineering, credited in canon to designer Walex Blissex and fielded by the Republic in the last days of the Clone Wars. Deployed in swarms from Venator-class carriers, it fought at the Battle of Coruscant and then became the Imperial Navy's primary starfighter during the Empire's first years.\n\nFast and agile, the wedge-shaped V-wing mounted twin rapid-fire laser cannons and vertical folding radiator wings on articulated collars, but carried no hyperdrive and, to save mass, no pressurized cockpit. Pilots flew in sealed life-support suits, assisted by a Q7 astromech droid. That austere, carrier-dependent philosophy directly prefigured TIE doctrine.\n\nCanon sources name the V-wing, alongside the Eta-2, as a model Raith Sienar drew upon when creating the TIE line, and its twin vertical wing arrangement is an obvious visual ancestor. V-wings soldiered on beside early TIEs until the TIE/ln superseded them by the time of the Galactic Civil War.",
  "advproject": "The Advanced Project ship is a Legends design known only from prose in the novel Rogue Planet — no official artwork exists, so any rendering is fan interpretation. Raith Sienar's Republic Sienar Systems was completing this latest, smallest experimental model by 29 BBY, pre-sold to corporate clients including the Trade Federation as a freighter escort for the Outer Rim Territories.\n\nDescriptions give it a compact spherical crew compartment without the Scimitar's bladed forward hull, broad vertical cooling vanes across a twenty-meter span, a black finish, and the ability to deploy from tightly packed launch stacks in standard cargo holds — essentially the TIE's signature look years early. Per The Star Wars Sourcebook, the design was expanded into the T.I.E. starfighter and its successor designs.",
  "tie_proto": "This node merges two takes on the first TIE. In canon, the TIE fighter prototype was designed by Raith Sienar of Sienar Fleet Systems at the behest of Grand Moff Tarkin and the Navy Admiralty, after Emperor Palpatine approved an unshielded fighter concept. Sienar drew on the Eta-2 and V-wing; an initial airframe unsuited to atmosphere was superseded by a strengthened-spar version chosen for production.\n\nIn Legends, the Twin Ion Engine (T.I.E.) starfighter was a Republic-era Sienar design — the first fighter with the famous ball cockpit flanked by vertical wings — built around the radical SIE-TIE twin ion engine unveiled shortly before the Battle of Geonosis. It answered a Republic drive for a cheap, standard fighter that could stack in the cargo holds and launch racks of existing cruisers.\n\nThe new engine was incompatible with contemporary hyperdrive, shield, and life-support systems, locking in the line's austere character. In both continuities this craft is the hinge of the tree: courier and Jedi-fighter DNA enters here, and mass-produced Imperial fighters — the TIE starfighter and ultimately the TIE/ln — come out.",
  "tie_sf": "The Legends TIE starfighter was the first upgraded model of the T.I.E. produced under the Galactic Empire. Sienar Fleet Systems impressed Palpatine with a fighter costing a fraction of rival proposals — about 75,000 credits new — and it served through the Empire's early years before the cheaper TIE/ln displaced it; by 0 ABY most examples had been replaced or scrapped.\n\nUnlike the later TIE/ln, it ran engines and weapons off one centralized generator, and it was better equipped than its successor: weak shields, a small hyperdrive with a two-jump navicomputer, and two single-shot concussion missile launchers let it double as a light bomber. But those systems clashed with the twin ion engine, requiring awkward secondary power, and it was slower with weaker laser cannons.\n\nThe Force Unleashed Campaign Guide's \"TIE Prototype\" entry is nearly identical and is treated as another name for this craft. The type's real legacy was economic: the Empire stripped out its secondary generators and extras to create the enormously mass-produced TIE/ln.",
  "imp_proto": "This Legends prototype — its name conjectural — appears in Darth Vader and the Ghost Prison #1, under development within the first months of the Empire in 19 BBY. It paired the familiar spherical command pod, top entry hatch, and solar-paneled wings with one oddity: the wings were mounted horizontally rather than vertically, giving it a distinctly transitional look.\n\nIts escape system was infamous: a catapult unit under the pilot's seat that sometimes exploded and vaporized the occupant, or slammed him into the pod roof when the access hatch failed to open. Lieutenant Laurita Tohm was sealed inside one by friends to keep him clear of Headmaster Gentis's coup, and ejected only after weighing those risks.\n\nBehind the scenes, the craft strongly resembles Alex Jaeger's unused \"clone pod fighter\" concept art for Revenge of the Sith. Wookieepedia files it among TIE prototypes, but no manufacturer, statistics, or armament were ever published, so its exact place in the lineage rests on visual similarity alone.",
  "infiltrator": "The TIE infiltrator is one of the thinnest entries in the entire TIE family tree. It is known solely from a passing mention in the 2013 Death Star Owner's Technical Manual, which describes it as a TIE-series starfighter developed by Raith Sienar of Sienar Fleet Systems and derived from the original T.I.E. fighter.\n\nNo image, statistics, armament, or service history were ever published, and its date is unknown. The name implies a covert-operations role, but no source elaborates, so anything beyond that single-sentence description — including its placement in fan lineages like this one — is extrapolation rather than established lore.",
  "tieln": "The TIE/ln space superiority starfighter is the definitive Imperial fighter: the howling, ball-and-hexagon silhouette seen in every corner of the galaxy. Designed by Raith Sienar and mass-produced by Sienar Fleet Systems, it was in canon service by 18 BBY (Legends places its design after 22 BBY) and remained the Navy's standard fighter throughout the Galactic Civil War.\n\nEverything served cost and expendability: roughly 60,000 credits bought twin L-s1 (later L-s7.2) laser cannons, twin ion engines fed by twelve solar collector panels, and superb agility — but no shields, hyperdrive, or life support, forcing pilots into sealed suits and keeping fighters tethered to carriers, which racked them 72 to a Star Destroyer.\n\nDoctrinally it embodied quantity over quality, attacking in overwhelming swarms. In Legends it refined the earlier TIE starfighter with separate generators for guns and engines; in canon it distilled the V-wing and Eta-2 inheritance. Every advanced TIE on this tree is a reaction to the TIE/ln's cheap, brilliant, fragile baseline.",
  "adv_prototype": "The TIE Advanced v1 was an experimental Sienar Fleet Systems model unveiled on Empire Day of 4 BBY on Lothal, as seen in Star Wars Rebels. Though presented for elite pilots like Baron Valen Rudor, the prototypes were largely appropriated by the Inquisitorius — the Grand Inquisitor, Fifth Brother, and Seventh Sister all hunted Jedi from their cockpits.\n\nCanon describes it as heavily based on Sienar's own Scimitar. It broke TIE convention with basic deflector shields and a hyperdrive, folding wings that conserved hangar space, solar panels on the inner wing faces only (armor outboard), stronger chin-mounted laser cannons, and a launcher for missiles and S-thread tracking devices. It cost about 150,000 credits.\n\nRebel analysts in The Rebel Files judged the TIE Advanced x1 to be \"the next generation of the v1,\" fixing this fighter's spot between the TIE/ln and Vader's craft. Legends had earlier sketched an equivalent \"TIE Advanced prototype\" commissioned from Raith Sienar, mentioned in the Imperial Handbook.",
  "advx1": "The TIE Advanced x1 is Darth Vader's fighter from A New Hope: a limited-production Sienar Fleet Systems prototype with an elongated rear deck, reinforced durasteel hull, and distinctive bent-wing solar arrays. It carried what standard TIEs lacked — a deflector shield system and a modest class-4 hyperdrive — plus twin heavy L-s9.3 laser cannons and a cluster missile launcher.\n\nThe added mass cost maneuverability, traded for battle resilience; Legends notes its advanced targeting system needed constant tuning and that it still omitted life support. In Legends, Vader personally commissioned the design from Raith Sienar, but the Navy balked at mass orders, citing cost — and privately fearing hyperdrive-equipped fighters would undercut capital-ship budgets and ease defections. Only a few squadrons formed.\n\nIts influence outran its numbers. Legends credits the x1's bent-wing innovation to the TIE bomber and TIE Interceptor, and continued Advanced development led toward the TIE Avenger and TIE Defender. In canon it remained a favorite of elite units like Storm Squadron, while Vader flew his through Yavin and beyond.",
  "sentinel": "The TIE Sentinel is a genuine obscurity: it exists because of a single in-game painting, \"Emperor's Eyes,\" in Star Wars Galaxies: An Empire Divided, whose caption names the craft. The Legends fighter depicted has irregular folded wings and at least two dual-gun turrets — one dorsal, one at the chin — a highly unusual arrangement for a TIE-series design.\n\nWookieepedia notes it shared traits with the TIE Advanced x1 and the TIE Aggressor and was at least planned by the Empire in the aftermath of the Battle of Yavin. It was never a usable craft in the game, and no statistics or service history exist; related concept art appears in From Pencil to Pixel: The Art of Star Wars Galaxies.",
  "x1mod": "Per Star Wars Galaxies: Rage of the Wookiees and its accompanying developer feature, Darth Vader had the TIE Advanced x1 line extensively modified sometime after the Battle of Yavin. The visible result was a variant with flaps along the wing edges; rumor held that the last iteration also added solar strakes over the existing panels to harvest additional power.\n\nThat extra power reportedly fed an experimental engine/booster combination that was never released to ordinary pilots, though the modified airframes themselves were flown by high-ranking Imperial Navy starfighter pilots. It remains a Legends footnote from an MMO expansion — a reminder that x1 development continued quietly after Vader's famous prototype flew at Yavin.",
  "x1var": "No official article describes a distinct \"TIE Advanced x1 variant\"; this entry reflects early comic artwork whose bent-wing fighters do not quite match the film model. Marvel's Star Wars (1977) run drew x1-style craft in ordinary Imperial squadron service — for instance the staged attack on the Wheel — with proportions and details that drift from the screen-used design.\n\nWookieepedia's own notes point the same direction: Geoffrey Mandel's 1978 Star Destroyer blueprints include bent-wing \"TIE Assault Craft\" that may be renamed x1s or a possible sister craft. In short, the \"variant\" is best understood as period Legends art of the x1 lineage rather than a separately documented starfighter, and nothing concrete about it can honestly be stated.",
  "avenger": "In Legends, the TIE/ad starfighter — nicknamed TIE Avenger to avoid confusion with Vader's x1 — was the Advanced line's production culmination, developed around 3 ABY under Admiral Zaarin. It combined four wingtip L-s9.3 laser cannons, two general-purpose warhead launchers, Novaldex deflector shields, an ND9 hyperdrive enabled by a Habeen breakthrough, and speed and agility exceeding any contemporary fighter.\n\nEnemy pilots called them \"brights.\" Their combat debut was ignominious — traitor Admiral Harkov's Avengers first flew against loyalist forces — and Zaarin later destroyed the production plants, so the 400,000-credit fighter stayed rare, overshadowed by the TIE Defender even as aces like Maarek Stele preferred it.\n\nCanon reinvented it via Andor: the TIE Avenger prototype, a large, heavily armed, shielded and hyperdrive-equipped testbed stolen by Cassian Andor from Sienar Test Facility 73 in 4 BBY — a very different dating from Legends. The canon Databank ties it to post-x1 development alongside the TIE Interceptor; exorbitant cost again prevented mass production.",
  "advx2": "The TIE/x2 was a Legends prototype from Sienar Fleet Systems' TIE Mod Team, first flying around 0 ABY. It folded the TIE Advanced x1's innovations into a new package: higher-performance ion engines than the TIE/ln, a hyperdrive, improved combat software, a hull that departed from TIE-series norms, and inward-swept solar/radiator panels giving the pilot much better visibility.\n\nThirty-two airframes underwent nine months of field trials under veteran TIE wing commanders, shadowed by diagnostic droids and engineering teams, before returning to Lianna. Despite glowing pilot reports, Imperial Defense Daily claimed it would not see active duty; commentators suspect it is the \"TIE Advanced\" seen in the X-Wing video game under another name.",
  "advx3": "The TIE/x3 was the TIE Mod Team's follow-on testbed, first noted in 2 ABY in the Adventure Journal's Galaxywide NewsNets — effectively its only appearance, so the record is thin. It carried forward the x-series improvements: ion engines outperforming the standard TIE/ln's, upgraded combat software, and the inward-swept solar/radiator panels configured for pilot visibility.\n\nIt was also a retreat toward orthodoxy: its hullform returned to conventional TIE fighter lines rather than the experimental shapes of the x1 and x2, and it deleted their hyperdrives entirely. That cheaper, more conservative direction reads as Sienar testing which advanced features the cost-conscious Imperial Navy would actually accept in a fleet fighter.",
  "advx7": "The TIE Advanced x7 is the missing link before the Defender, attested in both continuities. Canon's Build the Millennium Falcon magazine states the earliest TIE/D Defenders were developed from x7 prototypes: six launched during the Battle of Hoth in 3 ABY, and though all were destroyed, Sienar Fleet Systems harvested crucial data — a dating that sits awkwardly beside Rebels' 2 BBY Defender program.\n\nLegends gives more detail: also called the TIE/Ad Defender or TIE/x7, it was built on Corulag shortly before Endor as a radical departure — three solar collectors spaced evenly around the cockpit pod, triple maneuvering-jet arrays, four fire-linked laser cannons, and far greater speed than standard TIEs. It still lacked the ion cannons, missile launchers, and hyperdrive that would define the finished TIE Defender.",
  "defender": "The TIE/d \"Defender\" Multi-Role Starfighter was Sienar's tri-winged apex predator: deflector shields, a class-2 hyperdrive, agility and speed roughly 40 percent beyond a TIE/ln, and crushing armament — laser cannons (canon counts six wingtip plus two chin guns; Legends four), two ion cannons, twin warhead launchers, and an optional tractor beam — at roughly 300,000 credits apiece.\n\nIn Legends it emerged shortly before Endor under Grand Admiral Zaarin, whose defection turned his own Defenders against the Empire; Thrawn's counter-campaign contained the technology. Reserved for elite veterans and nicknamed \"trips\" and \"X-wing killers,\" it served the Imperial Remnant for decades, fighting as late as the Yuuzhan Vong War.\n\nCanon rewrote its origin: designed by Morgan Elsbeth and championed by Thrawn's Lothal initiative from 2 BBY, with Vader's endorsement producing the faster Defender Elite. Krennic's Project Stardust ultimately swallowed its funding after the Lothal fuel-depot disaster, and the program died — though its ideas resurfaced in First Order fighters.",
  "redstar1": "Red Star 1 was an individual starfighter, not a class: the personal TIE/D Defender of Countess Iran Ryad, who led the Imperial Red Star Squadron in Legends. Painted in a signature red scheme, it carried an improved deflector shield generator, an upgraded ion engine, and an enhanced fire-control system over the already formidable stock Defender.\n\nThe Wizards of the Coast web article \"More Starships!\" — essentially its only source — values it at 262,500 credits against the 200,000 it quotes for a standard Defender, a figure that conflicts with the usual 300,000-credit price. Ryad was charged with treason and shot down with her fighter around 5 ABY by Baron Soontir Fel; she later resurfaced as a pilot card in the X-Wing miniatures game.",
  "interceptor": "The TIE/IN interceptor was Sienar Fleet Systems' premier dogfighter of the Galactic Civil War, instantly recognizable by its four dagger-shaped solar panels. Nicknamed the 'Squint,' it traded the TIE/ln's hexagonal wings for a narrower, more menacing profile that widened the pilot's field of view while shrinking the target it presented to enemy gunners.\n\nEnlarged P-s5.6 twin ion engines and an uprated solar ionization reactor pushed it to roughly 1,250 kph, with agility only the Rebel A-wing could match. Armament centered on four wingtip L-s9.3 laser cannons; like most TIEs it carried no shields or hyperdrive, relying on speed, numbers, and elite units such as the 181st Imperial Fighter Wing.\n\nIn canon, a prototype flew by 14 BBY and official deployment came in 3 BBY, as a cheaper mass-production alternative to the TIE Advanced x1's bent-wing design; Legends instead dates its debut to around 1 BBY as a counter to the X-wing. By Endor it formed a fifth of the Imperial fighter fleet, and it seeded successors from the Royal Guard variant to, in Legends, the Predator-class.",
  "crimson": "Almost nothing concrete is recorded about this crimson-painted TIE/IN interceptor variant, which carries only a conjectural name on Wookieepedia. Four of the fighters escorted the Lambda-class shuttle carrying Emperor Palpatine and Darth Vader during the Battle at Amaxine Station, fighting alongside Royal Guards and death troopers, their hulls matching the Guard's signature red.\n\nThe craft debuted in Star Wars: Squadrons and appeared in the Hidden Empire comic, making it effectively the current-canon counterpart of Legends' Royal Guard TIE Interceptor. Whether it carried that craft's shield and hyperdrive upgrades, or was simply a repainted interceptor flown by elite escort pilots, is never stated — the sources honestly show it only as a striking bodyguard craft.",
  "m3": "The TIE Experimental M3, informally the 'TIE Warhead,' was one of the oddball fighters produced by the secret TIE Experimental Project, a Legends research effort run by Director Lenzer under Grand Admiral Demetrius Zaarin. Introduced in the 1999 game Star Wars: X-Wing Alliance, it appeared around 3 ABY and vanished just as quickly.\n\nThe M3 was essentially a TIE Interceptor with its laser cannons deleted and two warhead launchers mounted on the outer wing hubs, feeding a standard load of eight concussion missiles. Most examples also gained shields at little performance cost. Strangest of all, it was pilotless — slaved by remote control to a modified Beta-class ETR-3 escort transport.\n\nRebel forces lured out and destroyed the drone fighters and their vulnerable control ships, and after the Alliance demolished the project's production facilities, no TIE Experimentals were seen again — a dead-end branch of the Interceptor family, but a preview of Imperial interest in expendable, missile-armed drone fighters.",
  "royal_guard": "The Emperor's Royal Guard TIE/IN starfighter was a crimson-painted, heavily upgraded TIE Interceptor flown exclusively by Palpatine's Royal Guard, most often escorting the Emperor's shuttle and its decoys. Said to have been commissioned by Palpatine himself, the type first appeared in Star Wars Galaxies: Rage of the Wookiees.\n\nUnder Darth Vader's supervision, Imperial technicians fitted each fighter with a built-in hyperdrive and shield generator plus additional flaps on the curves of the wings — and, unusually, the craft came out faster and more agile than the stock Interceptor, unlike ordinary field-modified TIEs weighed down by similar equipment.\n\nGuard fighter wings were rumored to rival the 181st and Rogue Squadron. The design is Legends material, but its imagery persists: canon's crimson interceptor escorts at Amaxine Station fill the identical role, and Wookieepedia pairs the two articles as continuity counterparts.",
  "starhunter": "The Starhunter is one of the most thinly documented ships in the TIE line: it appears only in the 2008 novel Legacy of the Force: Invincible, which describes it as a further evolution of the TIE/IN interceptor serving the Imperial Remnant's navy late in the Second Galactic Civil War, around 41 ABY.\n\nThe novel credits it with a deflector shield generator and heavy weapons alongside its laser cannons, flown by a single pilot at engagements like the Second Battle of Roche and Uroro Station. Even its name is uncertain — the text simply calls it 'Starhunter.' No manufacturer, dimensions, or hyperdrive rating were ever published, so its exact position between the Interceptor and later designs rests on that one line of prose.",
  "predator": "The Predator-class fighter — 'TIE Predator,' still tagged 'eyeballs' by Galactic Alliance pilots — was Sienar Fleet Systems' frontline fighter for the Fel Empire in the Legends Legacy era, appearing from the first 2006 Legacy comic set in 130 ABY. It served through the Sith–Imperial War and the Second Imperial Civil War, flown by Krayt's Sith Empire, Roan Fel's Empire-in-exile, and the Imperial Knights.\n\nConsidered the successor to the century-old TIE Interceptor, it finally fixed the line's classic weaknesses: a Class 1 hyperdrive, navigation computer, and deflector shields generated through its blade-shaped wings. Those articulated wings, echoing the Chiss Clawcraft, adjusted position in flight and folded for docking, though the technology packed inside made maintenance a nightmare. Four L-s9.3 laser cannons provided firepower.\n\nThe redesigned slatted viewport gave such poor visibility that the Navy lobbied Sienar into building squadron-leader variants with classic TIE cockpits — fans initially mistook the slatted craft for droid fighters. The type soldiered on until the Sith-Imperial starfighter began supplanting it in Krayt's forces.",
  "neutralizer": "The Neutralizer-class bomber was a Legends craft developed by Sienar Fleet Systems during the Sith–Imperial War (which began in 127 ABY) by adapting the Predator-class fighter's design. It served the Fel Empire and later Darth Krayt's Galactic Empire, though comparatively few were built; it exists only in the 2009 Legacy Era Campaign Guide and never appeared on a comic page.\n\nLarger, bulkier, and less agile than the Predator, it kept the Class 1 hyperdrive and navicomputer and mounted unusually strong shields for its size, while remaining nimble for a bomber at 1,500 kph in atmosphere. A single pilot wielded two medium laser cannons and ten proton torpedoes. Its specialty was strafing ground emplacements and cracking fortified positions — including, under Krayt, punitive air strikes on locations suspected of sheltering Roan Fel loyalists.",
  "sith_imp": "The Sith-Imperial starfighter — a conjectural name — was the fighter Darth Krayt's Galactic Empire fielded to replace the Predator-class during the Second Imperial Civil War, debuting in Legacy #45 in 137 ABY. Darth Rauder, leader of the First Sith Imperial Strikeforce's pilots, called it 'the latest design and a symbol of the Sith Empire.'\n\nVisually it kept clear TIE-series DNA while strongly resembling the Chiss Nssis-class Clawcraft, with a distinctive quad-wing layout. It first flew in Operation: Thunderstroke, replacing Skull Squadron's TIE Predators, and fought at the Devastation of Da Soocha and the Attack on the Hidden Temple. Beyond laser cannons and a single pilot, no technical specifications were ever published — this Legends craft is the last known branch of the TIE family tree.",
  "hunter": "The TIE/hu 'Hunter' Multi-Role Starfighter was a Legends design from Sienar Fleet Systems, developed between 1 BBY and 0 BBY specifically to counter the Rebel Alliance's X-wing. Debuting in Rogue Squadron III: Rebel Strike, it was reserved exclusively for the Empire's storm commandos, with Crix Madine warning Wedge Antilles that the 'new TIE Hunters' were deadly.\n\nIts wing configuration roughly reversed the Interceptor's, and — copying its Rebel target — it mounted X-wing-style S-foils that opened for attack. Unusually for a wartime TIE it carried both shields and a hyperdrive, and its loadout of two laser cannons, two ion cannons, and twelve proton torpedoes rivaled a Y-wing's. It was reputedly the fastest TIE in the fleet, and even waterproof.\n\nPer the sourcebook Stay on Target, the Hunter was field-tested by then-Captain Soontir Fel, tying it to the same program as the Aggressor, Defender, and Phantom. Production lagged behind the Navy's wishes, and captured examples let Rogue Squadron raid the Fondor shipyards before Endor.",
  "aggressor": "The TIE/ag 'Aggressor' Starfighter, which exists in both canon and Legends, broke the TIE mold by adding a second crewman: its elongated command pod housed a gunner operating a turret-mounted twin light laser cannon. Originating in Star Wars Galaxies: Jump to Lightspeed (2004), it entered canon via the X-Wing miniatures game and the Starships and Speeders sourcebook.\n\nA hybrid fighter-bomber, it paired the dorsal turret with forward-mounted medium laser cannons and ordnance — concussion missiles in Legends, barrage rockets in canon — while retaining classic TIE austerity: no shields, no hyperdrive, no navicomputer. Introduced shortly before Yavin against Rebel-aligned smuggling and shipping, it excelled at commerce raiding, patrol, and surveillance, including at Scarif.\n\nIn Legends its development was overseen at the Imperial Classified Flight Yards by then-Captain Soontir Fel alongside the Hunter, Defender, and Phantom. Pilots prized its reliability and long-mission independence, but expense and training demands kept it semi-experimental and produced in limited numbers.",
  "reaper": "The TIE/rp Reaper attack lander is a canon troop-carrier branch of the TIE family, introduced on screen in Rogue One (2016). Built by Sienar Fleet Systems, it was designed to ferry elite troops to battlefield flashpoints — most famously delivering Orson Krennic's death troopers against Rogue One during the Battle of Scarif, escorted by TIE strikers.\n\nAt 24.2 meters, with streamlined horizontal wings flanking a large hinged central stabilizer, the Reaper dwarfs a normal TIE. It carried a crew of two plus ten passengers and, rarely for the line, mounted deflector shields, a Class 1 hyperdrive, life support, and ejector seats, with two L-s9.3 laser cannons for self-defense.\n\nCanon has pushed it well beyond Rogue One: the Inquisitor Ninth Sister used one as a personal transport by 14 BBY in Jedi: Fallen Order, Reapers hunt escapees in Andor, Doctor Aphra stole one on Tython, and the criminal Ranc Gang still flew them decades after Endor.",
  "striker": "The TIE/sk x1 experimental air superiority fighter — the TIE striker — is the canon TIE line's atmospheric specialist, introduced in Rogue One defending Scarif. Per The Galactic Explorer's Guide it emerged from the Empire's Scarif think tank, and it flew as early as the 18 BBY Empire Day parade, making it one of the few TIEs used by the Imperial Army as well as the Navy.\n\nIts flat, pointed high-speed wings could curl downward to shift from flight mode to attack mode, boosting speed toward 1,500 kph and aiding vertical take-off and landing. Armament was heavy: four fire-linked L-s9.3 laser cannons, two H-s1 heavy cannons, and a belly chute for VL-61/79 proton bombs. True to TIE tradition, it carried no shields or hyperdrive.\n\nAn enlarged central pod let it haul supplies or an optional bombardier, and it could fight in space, if awkwardly. Strikers dueled X-wings over Scarif, defended Imperial installations throughout the war, and were still fighting the New Republic at Jakku in 5 ABY.",
  "dagger": "The Dagger-class starfighter is a Legends fighter from Republic Sienar Systems — Sienar's pre-Imperial ancestor — produced in large numbers around the Battle of Naboo (32 BBY) and introduced in the 2001 game Star Wars: Starfighter. Sold mostly to planetary security forces, thousands also reached pirates and mercenaries, including those hired against Naboo by the Trade Federation.\n\nIts signature was a triple wing of pointed, knife-like panels; the whole craft rotated around its fixed cockpit in flight. Three hull-mounted laser cannons, a Class 2 hyperdrive, high speed, and a 35,000-credit price made it dangerous and cheap — but it had no shields, no life support, and no inertial dampeners, leaving pilots at the mercy of raw g-forces.\n\nProduction ended when Palpatine dissolved Republic Sienar, but successor Sienar Fleet Systems borrowed elements of the Dagger in its initial TIE-series work, and the design later served directly as the basis for the V38 assault fighter and, through it, the TIE Phantom.",
  "v38": "The V38 assault fighter is an obscure Legends craft, mentioned rather than starring in Rebel Assault II and a handful of sourcebooks. Developed by the Galactic Empire on the planet Imdaar during the Galactic Civil War, it is remembered almost entirely as the airframe from which the TIE Phantom was built — later sources even call it a 'failed' fighter.\n\nWhat is recorded makes it unique among TIEs: its quadanium steel solar wings attached directly to the hull rather than via pylons, its cockpit was non-spherical — closer to the Scimitar assault bomber — and its triple-wing arrangement recalled both the TIE Defender and the old Dagger-class. Admiral Ackbar recognized the type from footage of TIE Phantoms in action. Nothing else — armament, speed, production numbers — was ever published, so honesty requires leaving its stat sheet blank.",
  "phantom": "The TIE/ph 'Phantom' Multi-Role Stealth Starfighter was the Empire's cloaked fighter: a modified V38 assault fighter fitted with a Sienar Mk. III stygium cloaking device. In Legends it was the fruit of Grand Admiral Martio Batch's secret project on Imdaar Alpha; Batch shattered the planet Aeten II with the Tarkin superweapon to harvest the stygium crystals the cloak required.\n\nBeyond invisibility to eye and sensor, the two-man Phantom carried deflector shields, a Class 1 hyperdrive, life support, and five laser cannons — three on its Defender-like tri-wing tips, two under the elongated cockpit. Cloaking drained weapons and hyperdrive, so an automated system decloaked for each volley and re-cloaked instantly. Every 365,000-credit fighter hid a self-destruct to protect the technology.\n\nIn Rebel Assault II, Rebel pilots Rookie One and Ru Murleen stole a Phantom and destroyed both the Super Star Destroyer Terror and the Imdaar factory, ending the 'Phantom fleet.' The design was reintroduced to canon via Star Wars: Armada and Starships and Speeders with much the same profile: cloak, shields, hyperdrive, and extreme rarity.",
  "m1": "The TIE Experimental M1, nicknamed the 'TIE Bizarro' by Rebel pilots, was a Legends-continuity prototype from the video game Star Wars: X-Wing Alliance. It belonged to the secret TIE Experimental Project, run by Director Lenzer under Grand Admiral Demetrius Zaarin, which sought to develop remote-piloted TIE craft as a step toward fully automated fighters.\n\nIts layout was genuinely bizarre: a single standard TIE/LN wing panel braced between two TIE cockpit pods. The port pod carried control systems while the starboard pod mounted a turbolaser, a capital-ship-grade weapon far heavier than any prior TIE armament. Both pods retained twin ion engines, making it slightly faster than a stock TIE/LN, and its hyperdrive was slaved to a controller ship.\n\nFlown remotely by pilots aboard modified escort transports, M1s raided commercial shipping in the Vilonis sector around 3 ABY to gather combat data. The Rebel Alliance first fought them near the Belat system, and the type vanished after the destruction of the project's research station Obsidian.",
  "m2": "The TIE Experimental M2, dubbed the 'TIE BigGun,' was another Legends prototype of the TIE Experimental Project from X-Wing Alliance, built by Sienar Fleet Systems for Director Lenzer's remote-fighter program under Grand Admiral Zaarin around 3 ABY.\n\nMechanically it was the most conservative of the experimentals: a standard TIE/LN hull with the chin-mounted laser cannons deleted and a pair of turbolaser turrets fitted to the outer wing hubs, with optional light shielding. Speed roughly matched the TIE/LN, though maneuverability suffered slightly. Like its siblings it had no pilot aboard, being flown remotely from a modified Beta-class escort transport.\n\nThe Alliance captured an intact M2 using a decoy convoy screened by Z-95s armed with ion pulse warheads, though the controller crews died to rigged life support before interrogation. M2s later defended the project's facilities, and none were seen after the fall of the station Obsidian.",
  "m5": "The TIE Experimental M5, the 'TIE Booster,' rounded out the TIE Experimental Project's known airframes in Legends continuity, appearing in X-Wing Alliance. Like its stablemates it was developed by Lenzer's team under Grand Admiral Zaarin as a remotely piloted testbed for future Imperial droid fighters.\n\nThe M5 was essentially a stock TIE/LN with a large booster engine welded to the aft of the ball cockpit, stretching it to ten meters. The result hit 132 MGLT, far beyond a standard TIE, at a modest cost in agility. It kept twin laser cannons, carried a hyperdrive slaved to its control ship, and many airframes added shield generators with no performance loss.\n\nRebels first met the M5 during a retaliatory Imperial strike on the cruiser Liberty; one pilot famously described it as an engine pack welded to a simple TIE. The type disappeared with the destruction of research station Obsidian around 3 ABY.",
  "oppressor": "The TIE Oppressor was a Legends-continuity strike fighter introduced in Star Wars Galaxies: Jump to Lightspeed, deployed in limited numbers by the Imperial Navy shortly after the Battle of Yavin. Sienar Fleet Systems designed it as a light bomber that could still dogfight, an Imperial answer to the Rebel Y-wing.\n\nThe design crammed heavy weapons, armor, and shielding onto a TIE-derived spaceframe: two L-s9.3 laser cannons plus missile launchers and projectile mounts, with enlarged solar arrays to feed it all. Because it kept the same basic engines as earlier TIEs on a much heavier hull, pilots rated its maneuverability as merely average, though it remained nimble compared with other bombers.\n\nFlown only by veteran pilots under a 'Limited Fielding Test Initiative,' the Oppressor could be lethal in anti-starfighter trim, using its sluggish reputation to bait overconfident enemies. As Imperial funds declined after the Galactic Civil War, the expensive type is believed to have faded away.",
  "automated": "The TIE/D automated starfighter, or droid TIE, was a pilotless TIE flown by the reborn Emperor Palpatine's Dark Empire in 10 ABY. It originates in the Dark Empire comics and the Rogue Squadron video game, Legends material, though it also appeared in the old StarWars.com Databank. Sienar Fleet Systems based it directly on the TIE/LN.\n\nThe TIE/D kept the standard command pod, twin ion engines, and twin laser cannons, but added extra armor and rectangular, pitch-adjustable wing panels. In place of a pilot sat a ten-kilogram Cybot Galactica Ace-6 droid brain, which freed mass for improved protection. Fast, small, and utterly expendable, each unit cost about 170,000 credits.\n\nGeneral Arndall Lott developed the concept after slaved remote-control experiments proved jammable, drawing instead on Clone Wars droid starfighters. World Devastator factories churned out swarms at the Battle of Calamari, but New Republic pilots outwitted the drones, and production largely died with the Devastators.",
  "vulture": "The Vulture-class starfighter, or vulture droid, was the mass-produced droid starfighter of the Trade Federation and later the Confederacy of Independent Systems during the Clone Wars. Built by Haor Chall Engineering in the cathedral factories of the Xi Char, it exists in both canon and Legends with largely consistent depictions.\n\nHaving no pilot or life support freed space for weapons and fuel: quad blaster cannons and energy torpedo launchers on an insectoid frame that could transform into a walking patrol mode and cling to hulls in zero gravity. Early models were slaved to Lucrehulk-class droid control ships; the type was cheap, hyperdrive-less, and designed to swarm, trading individual cunning for overwhelming numbers.\n\nIn this tech tree it matters less as a TIE relative than as a doctrinal ancestor: Imperial researchers explicitly studied Clone Wars droid starfighters when developing automated TIE projects such as the TIE/D.",
  "raptor": "The TIE/rpt starfighter, or TIE Raptor, was a Legends-continuity fighter introduced in the X-Wing: Wraith Squadron novels (Solo Command) and detailed in Cracken's Threat Dossier. It was an unofficial TIE: Warlord Zsinj's engineers designed and built it at his Dathomir shipyards after the Battle of Endor, with Sienar Fleet Systems contributing nothing but the original TIE lineage.\n\nThe Raptor kept the classic ball cockpit and octagonal viewport but replaced the big radiator wings entirely with four short fins projecting from the hull at right angles, shrinking the craft to 6.8 meters. Slower than a TIE Interceptor but as agile as an A-wing, it packed four laser cannons and two concussion missile launchers, with shields on some prototypes.\n\nBecause it was exclusive to Zsinj's faction, the Raptor saw little or no service with the mainline Imperial Navy, remaining a warlord's signature fighter of the post-Endor era.",
  "vigilant": "The 'TIE Vigilant' has no Wookieepedia article and no established in-universe history; the name circulates in fan lineages such as this one. The design is a fan-rendered interpretation drawn from Kenner's TIE fighter toy line of the original-trilogy era, marketed alongside Return of the Jedi, whose simplified, stubby-winged proportions differed noticeably from the filming miniatures.\n\nBecause the source is a toy sculpt rather than a story, essentially nothing can be said about its manufacturer, armament, or service record without inventing it. It is best understood as a visual curiosity of the TIE/LN family: what the standard fighter looked like filtered through 1980s merchandising, later reimagined by fans as if it were a distinct variant.",
  "mining_guild": "The TIE/mg Mining Guild starfighter is a canon variant introduced in Star Wars Rebels ('The Call'), flown before 3 BBY. The Empire supplied surplus TIE/ln fighters to the Mining Guild, which modified them for corporate security and prospecting work, making them a rare example of TIEs in nominally civilian hands.\n\nThe modifications are distinctive: a yellow paint scheme to distinguish them from Imperial Navy craft, and a notch cut from each wing, leaving eight solar collectors instead of twelve. This improved cockpit visibility but noticeably degraded power generation and maneuverability. Unusually for a TIE, they carried basic shields, protecting the Guild's investment in hardware and pilot training, plus specialized sensors for detecting valuable ores and gases.\n\nTIE/mgs patrolled Guild territory against theft and scouted for resources. The crew of the Ghost fought them at an asteroid gas refinery, where Ezra Bridger, riding a purrgil, sliced a wing off one with his lightsaber.",
  "sentry": "The 'TIE Sentry' is an obscure design associated with the manga adaptation of A New Hope, and it has no Wookieepedia article of its own. The manga's artwork depicts TIE-type craft in a static guard role, which fans have treated as a distinct sentry variant of the TIE/LN.\n\nHonestly, almost nothing is established about it: no official name, statistics, manufacturer, or service history exist, and it is unclear whether the artist intended a new variant or simply drew standard TIE fighters loosely. As a manga-only design it sits at the fringes of Legends continuity, and any specifics beyond its appearance would be invention.",
  "iden": "This entry covers a specific airframe rather than a variant: the personal TIE/ln of Commander Iden Versio, canon protagonist of the Star Wars Battlefront II campaign. Versio was an elite Imperial Starfighter Corps pilot, flying as Sigma Three at the Battle of Yavin before leading Inferno Squad, the special forces unit assembled by her father, Admiral Garrick Versio.\n\nMechanically it is a standard Sienar Fleet Systems TIE/ln space superiority fighter; what set it apart was the pilot. In Battlefront II's starfighter modes her TIE appears as a hero ship with performance and loadout, afterburner and torpedo salvos, beyond a stock fighter, which is best read as game shorthand for an ace's finely tuned machine rather than a distinct production model.\n\nVersio flew it through actions from Yavin to the Empire's collapse, until she and Inferno Squad defected to the Rebellion after witnessing Operation: Cinder.",
  "light_duty": "The TIE Light Duty was a Legends-continuity trainer from Star Wars Galaxies: Jump to Lightspeed, used by the Galactic Empire during the Galactic Civil War. Rather than a new design, examples were typically reconditioned standard TIE fighters downgraded for instructional service.\n\nThe changes ran opposite to most variants in this tree: components were deliberately inferior, armor lighter, and firepower reduced, with limiters fitted to the solar ionization reactor and a secondary generator feeding its two L-s1 laser cannons. The stated logic was twofold: novice pilots could not do too much damage with it, and the gentler output spared fragile reactors and superstructures from student abuse.\n\nLittle else is recorded about the type; it exists mainly as the Empire's answer to the question of what rookie TIE pilots actually flew before graduating to frontline TIE/lns.",
  "super_tieln": "The Super TIE/LN starfighter, commonly the 'Super TIE,' was a Legends-continuity custom fighter from The Jedi Academy Sourcebook. It was created not by Sienar Fleet Systems but by weapons scientists at the top-secret Maw Installation, who modified the facility's complement of stock TIE/ln fighters to overcome the baseline design's shortcomings.\n\nExternally it looked almost identical to a standard TIE, distinguishable mainly by a slightly enlarged engine nacelle. Inside, it carried a more powerful engine and superior targeting systems, making it faster and deadlier than the fighter it was based on, capable of around 1,250 km/h while keeping the familiar twin laser cannons.\n\nThe Super TIE began as a hobby project, scientists disassembling and rebuilding TIEs for amusement, but dozens were eventually produced and used as interceptors against ships straying near the Maw. Developed in isolation, it was ultimately eclipsed by mainstream Imperial research, which produced the far more effective TIE Interceptor.",
  "fire_control": "The TIE/fc starfighter, 'fc' for fire control, was an Imperial reconnaissance and targeting variant of the TIE/LN. It originates in West End Games' 1987 Star Wars Sourcebook and related Legends material, with a mention in the old StarWars.com Databank; its placement here is undated because no introduction date is established.\n\nThe conversion traded firepower for eyes: one of the TIE/LN's two laser cannons was removed and replaced with a suite of sensors, leaving a single gun. Its core mission was scanning enemy bases and fleets and transmitting precise targeting data back to Imperial warships and bombers; the TIE/gt bomber in particular was designed to rely on TIE/fc target designation for accurate strikes.\n\nBecause the craft was so operationally valuable and so lightly armed, it was engineered to be highly maneuverable, surviving by agility while it painted targets for heavier hitters.",
  "brute": "The TIE/rb heavy starfighter, popularly the TIE Brute, is a canon variant introduced in Solo: A Star Wars Story, in service before 10 BBY. Sienar Fleet Systems developed it as the Empire pushed into the Outer Rim, wanting a TIE with capital-grade punch that would not depend on larger support vessels.\n\nWhen designers found the standard TIE airframe could not handle heavy H-s9.3 twin laser cannons, they mounted the guns in a pivoting, self-powered outrigger pod alongside the cockpit ball. That second pod also houses an MGK-300 integrated droid brain that assists the pilot like an astromech. The Brute adds heavy laminated armor, reinforced transparisteel, and, unusually for a TIE, life support, a pressurized cockpit, and an ejector seat, at the cost of speed.\n\nHan Solo flew one as a Carida cadet, and a Brute was the last pursuer the Millennium Falcon shook in the Akkadese Maelstrom. The First Order later fielded a successor model.",
  "stealth": "The TIE stealth was a Legends-continuity infiltration craft from Young Jedi Knights: Jedi Under Siege, used by the Second Imperium and Shadow Academy around 23 ABY. It was explicitly a modified TIE/ln, optimized to slip commandos through enemy detection while remaining fully combat-capable.\n\nIts additions were all about signature reduction: a secondary set of smaller, low-emission twin ion engines for quiet running, a sensor-dampening hull coating (possibly reflec), and landing gear for independent ground operations. It may also have carried enhanced sensors, as one threaded the narrow gap between the Jedi Praxeum's deflector shield and the treetops of Yavin 4.\n\nTwo TIE stealths opened the Second Imperium's assault on the Jedi academy, delivering the commandos Orvak and Dareb. After Orvak died to his own explosives, the teenaged Jaina Solo commandeered his fighter and shot down several Second Imperium craft before crash-landing, proving the type still fought like a TIE.",
  "scoutship": "This unnamed scout ship is a TIE-series model seen in the comic adaptation of Heir to the Empire, in service with Thrawn's forces by 9 ABY. Visually it is nearly indistinguishable from a standard TIE/LN; its one decisive difference is a hyperdrive, freeing it from carrier support for independent reconnaissance jumps.\n\nGrand Admiral Thrawn notably dispatched operatives in several of these craft to raid the Celebratus Archive library on Obroa-skai, the information raid that opens the Thrawn campaign. Beyond that mission, virtually nothing is recorded: no stats, armament, or designation survive, and even its identification as a distinct model rests on visual comparison in the comic.\n\nIt may be a later development of the TIE/rc reconnaissance fighter, which is plausible, a recon TIE finally given lightspeed legs, but unconfirmed in any source. It is a Legends design from licensed material adapting the novel.",
  "tie_recon": "The TIE/rc starfighter was the Empire's dedicated short-range reconnaissance TIE, created by West End Games for the Legends roleplaying line and in service before 2 BBY. It scouted for both fleet formations and starfighter wings, ranging ahead of forces into areas, such as asteroid fields, where capital ships could not operate.\n\nThe conversion from the TIE/LN swapped one of the two laser cannons for additional sensor and communications gear, leaving a single L-s1 cannon, while upgraded thrusters made it even more maneuverable than the baseline fighter. It carried no hyperdrive or shields, so standard doctrine had a carrier drop a TIE/rc squadron into a system and jump away; the TIEs raced through gathering data, then met the carrier at a rendezvous point.\n\nTIE/rcs served throughout the New Order era, appearing in several West End Games adventures, and spawned an upgraded descendant, the TIE/rc vanguard.",
  "vanguard": "The TIE/rc vanguard was a Legends-continuity upgrade of the TIE/rc reconnaissance fighter, first depicted on a 1996 Decipher customizable card game card and later detailed in the StarWars.com Databank. It served the Imperial Navy from at least the Battle of Yavin, entering service after 2 BBY.\n\nIt diverged further from the standard TIE silhouette than its predecessor: large antennae for sensor arrays and communications gear sprouted from the cockpit ball, and the upper section of each quadanium solar panel was shortened and swept outward to keep the wings clear of the dorsal scanners. Armament remained a single chin-mounted L-s1 laser cannon, but unlike the TIE/rc the vanguard carried limited deflector shields, protecting the intelligence it gathered as much as the pilot.\n\nVanguards were deployed ahead of fleet engagements to track enemy warship movements, the Empire's eyes before the hammer fell.",
  "tie_scout": "The name 'TIE scout' covers two related craft. In Legends, the TIE/sr starfighter was a limited-production long-range reconnaissance vessel, at 24 meters one of the largest TIE variants, with an elongated fuselage behind the cockpit, bent-wing solar panels, a crew of up to three, and a single laser cannon. Its defining feature was the best hyperdrive of any TIE, enabling extended independent scouting missions.\n\nSlow, unarmored, and expensive, the TIE/sr never saw mass production; Thrawn carried several aboard his flagship Admonitor in the Unknown Regions, and the type surveyed Outer Rim systems for minefields and ambushes during the Galactic Civil War. Its comprehensive com-scan suite could even ping hidden enemies.\n\nCanon has its own hyperdrive-equipped 'TIE scout' or scout-class TIE, mentioned in Alphabet Squadron and seen in The Rise of Skywalker, where Ben Solo flew one salvaged from the Death Star II wreckage on Kef Bir to reach Exegol in 35 ABY.",
  "lone_scout": "The Lone Scout-A was a Legends-continuity civilian scout vessel, a demilitarized sibling of the TIE/sr scout. Originally produced by Republic Sienar Systems, Sienar Fleet Systems' Republic-era predecessor, and later by Sienar and its civilian subsidiary Sienar Ships, it was among the first ships to use the revolutionary twin ion engine design, its cockpit foreshadowing the whole TIE line.\n\nBuilt for exploring the galaxy's wild regions, the 24-meter craft carried one crewer plus three passengers, 150 metric tons of cargo, and a full year of consumables, luxuries unthinkable in a military TIE. It mounted a Class 2 hyperdrive with a Class 15 backup, deflector shields, a single laser cannon, and a high-quality sensor array, albeit less sensitive than the TIE/sr's.\n\nUnlike the TIE series proper it sold openly on the civilian market, while also serving the Imperial Survey Corps and later the New Republic's exploration bureau. An improved Lone Scout A-2 followed.",
  "outland": "The Outland TIE fighter is a canon variant used by Imperial remnants after the Empire's fall, introduced in The Mandalorian from a Doug Chiang concept originally drawn for The Force Awakens. In flight it looks almost identical to a standard TIE/ln, but it was engineered for a fragmenting Empire that could no longer count on hangar racks and dedicated bases.\n\nIts solar panels fold upward at mid-span into a V-shape for landing, evoking an X-wing with open S-foils, while tripodal landing gear concealed in the hull lets it set down on uneven, unprepared ground and gives the pilot easy cockpit access. Armament remains a pair of L-s7.2 laser cannons.\n\nMoff Gideon flew a personal Outland TIE against Din Djarin and his allies on Nevarro in 9 ABY; Djarin brought it down with grav charges. More of the fighters stationed at Gideon's Nevarro base were later destroyed by the Razor Crest.",
  "gable": "Gable Karius was a canon Imperial Navy admiral appearing in the VR series Vader Immortal (2019), not, despite occasional fan attribution, the Shadow Wing-era TIE Fighter comic. A cyborg with a mechanical right arm and half-rebuilt face, he commanded an Interdictor-class Star Destroyer and the garrison of Fortress Vader on Mustafar.\n\nHis relevance to this tree is the machine he died in: a modified TIE/ln space superiority starfighter fitted with an extra turret on its left side, a one-off customization rather than a production variant. Nothing further about the fighter's specifications is established.\n\nWhen the reactivated Marching Horde droid army stormed Fortress Vader's hangar, Karius attacked the smuggler protagonist and the droid ZO-E3 in his custom TIE. The fighter was critically damaged during the fight and crashed and exploded in the hangar; sources say he 'seemingly' met his end, with death never explicitly confirmed.",
  "ground_target": "The TIE/gt (ground-targeting) starfighter was Sienar Fleet Systems' first dedicated bomber in the TIE line, fielded in the Empire's earliest years. It kept the standard ball cockpit and hexagonal solar panels of the TIE/LN, but enlarged and reinforced the hull, stretching the cockpit into a cylinder to fit an internal ordnance bay - the first TIE to trade the pure sphere for a payload-carrying pod.\n\nArmament was modest: one laser cannon plus a modular concussion missile launcher rated for twelve missiles, or alternate loads of six proton torpedoes, eighteen air-dropped mines, two cluster bombs, or twenty thousand propaganda leaflets. For pinpoint strikes it worked alongside TIE/fc fire-control craft, though large targets such as buildings and capital ships could be hit unaided.\n\nOnce the TIE/sa bomber arrived, the Navy purged its TIE/gts, pushing them to remote outposts and to Imperial Army ground-support squadrons that never really wanted them. The type originated in West End Games' Legends sourcebooks, and the designation also exists in current canon, making the gt the accepted missing link between TIE fighter and TIE bomber in both continuities.",
  "sa_bomber": "The TIE/sa (\"surface assault\") tactical bomber is the Empire's iconic dual-hulled bomber, built by Sienar Fleet Systems and introduced in the Empire's early years. First seen on screen in The Empire Strikes Back, it sets a starboard cockpit pod beside a portside ordnance pod between bent solar-array wings. Slower and bulkier than the TIE/LN, it earned nicknames like \"dupes\" and \"sitting ducks\" - but it could take a hit.\n\nThe ordnance pod was the point: proton torpedoes, concussion missiles, proton bombs, orbital mines, thermal detonators, even propaganda leaflets or a swap-in passenger cabin, dropped via a chute slaved to a T-s7b targeting computer and bomb sight. Two chin-mounted laser cannons provided fixed armament. Its single pilot enjoyed an ejector seat and life support - rare TIE luxuries - but no shields or hyperdrive.\n\nIn both canon and Legends it supplanted the TIE/gt as the fleet's standard bomber, serving from Ryloth and Hoth to the Great Purge of Mandalore, and lingering with Imperial remnants and even the First Order. Its modular twin-pod hull is the trunk of this tree, spawning the shuttle, boarding craft, lander, and the heavier bombers beyond.",
  "heavy_bomber": "The TIE Heavy Bomber is a Legends-only escalation of the TIE/sa, appearing solely in the 2001 strategy game Star Wars: Galactic Battlegrounds. Built by Sienar Fleet Systems for heavy assault and mobile-artillery work in space and atmosphere, it doubled the bomber's ordnance by carrying dual weapons pods in place of the original single payload module - other Legends material describes the second pod stacked atop the first.\n\nIn-game it specialized in destroying mechanized units, artillery like the T3-B heavy attack tank, and enemy power cores. Beyond that, honestly little is recorded: no crew figures, detailed armament, or service history survive, and no later source revisited the craft. Its chief significance to the lineage is as the plausible halfway step between the twin-pod TIE bomber and the four-podded TIE Punisher.",
  "interdictor": "The TIE/ca \"Punisher,\" also known as the TIE Interdictor or TIE/IT, is the heaviest expression of the TIE bomber's modular design. Produced by Sienar Fleet Systems after the Battle of Yavin, it flanks a bomber-style command module with paired ordnance pods - four in total - hauling roughly four times a TIE bomber's payload at a steep 253,000 credits per unit.\n\nUnusually for a mass-produced TIE, it carried deflector shields and reinforced armor, largely to guarantee its munitions arrived: linked laser cannons, torpedo or concussion-missile launchers, and twin proton bomb bays, with optional plasma and flechette torpedoes, cluster mines, and ion bombs. It flew heavy bombardment missions where Star Destroyers could not venture, hunting Rebel bases, though it still lacked a hyperdrive.\n\nThe craft spans both continuities: born in Legends via Galactic Battlegrounds (2001) and fleshed out by Fantasy Flight's X-Wing miniatures and RPG lines - which named pilots \"Redline\" and \"Deathrain\" - then carried into canon by the 2020 sourcebook Starships and Speeders.",
  "mark2": "The TIE Bomber Mark 2 exists only on paper, both in-universe and out. Legends' Star Wars Adventure Journal records that Captain Tomax Bren, ace of the elite Scimitar assault wing, proposed the upgrade after analyzing the TIE/sa's performance during the Hook Nebula campaign. His specifications called for greater speed, better maneuverability, and a less prominent profile than the bulky twin-hull bomber presented.\n\nThe Empire never approved production, but the proposal was not wasted: Grand Admiral Thrawn and Sienar Fleet Systems later incorporated several of Bren's suggestions into the Scimitar assault bomber. That makes the Mark 2 the documented conceptual bridge between the classic TIE bomber family and the Scimitar line. Nothing more about its intended layout or armament is known.",
  "scimitar_ab": "The Scimitar assault bomber was Sienar Fleet Systems' Thrawn-era rethink of Imperial bombing, a Legends craft put into production when the Grand Admiral approved a modified version of Tomax Bren's rejected TIE Bomber Mark 2 proposal. It abandoned the twin-pod TIE layout for a single elongated 13.8-meter fuselage, with a two-man command pod - pilot and bombardier - that could detach and serve as an escape pod.\n\nA single ion engine (hence no \"TIE\" prefix) worked with interlocked repulsorlift generators for agility, and unlike standard TIEs it carried shields. Armament comprised two laser cannons, sixteen missile racks holding thirty-two concussion missiles standard, plus chutes for thermal detonators and proton grenades. In atmosphere it cruised at 1,150 km/h and dive-bombed at 1,250 - faster than any TIE bomber.\n\nScimitars served the Empire and Imperial Remnant - Kir Kanos infamously destroyed the Star Destroyer Emperor's Revenge with one - and survived long enough that a full squadron ranked among the Galactic Alliance's elite in the final battle of the Yuuzhan Vong War.",
  "scimitar2": "The Scimitar Mark II Assault Bomber is a next-generation model of the Scimitar assault bomber, known from a single brief Legends entry in The Essential Guide to Vehicles and Vessels. It was reportedly identical to its predecessor in most respects, differing mainly in improved ion and repulsorlift engines that raised cruising speed to 1,150 km/h and dive-bombing speed to 1,250 km/h.\n\nBeyond that, nothing is recorded - no dates, users, or engagements - and confusingly, those \"improved\" figures match the speeds other sources give the original Scimitar, suggesting the guide's stats blur together. It stands here as the last documented twig of the TIE bomber branch, but honest summary requires saying it is little more than a footnote.",
  "terror": "The \"TIE Terror\" has no Wookieepedia article under that name, and neither \"TIE Terror\" nor \"Shadow Squad TIE\" is an official designation - the label comes from fan usage (including the Thrawn's Revenge game mod). The craft it points to is a nameless Legends TIE variant from the Star Wars: The Force Unleashed II graphic novel (circa 1 BBY), covered on Wookieepedia only as an \"unidentified TIE variant.\"\n\nWhat little is known: it used a triple-fuselage arrangement nearly identical to the TIE Heavy Bomber's, minus the ventral ordnance pod, with outward-canted wing panels recalling the TIE Advanced or Defender and wingtip weapons seen firing only ion cannons. Its port-side pods mounted large projectile launchers that fired unique boarding pods carrying the Empire's terror troopers and battle droids, and it was hyperdrive-equipped - a delivery system for the Terror Project's assault on the Rebel frigate Salvation. Anything beyond that single comic appearance is speculation.",
  "shuttle": "The TIE/sh VIP shuttle is the TIE bomber turned courier: a Sienar Fleet Systems variant that swaps the portside ordnance launcher and bomb chute for a small, well-appointed passenger pod seating up to four with a ton of cargo. It exists in both canon and Legends, and made its film debut in The Empire Strikes Back carrying Captain Needa to his fatal apology aboard Vader's Executor.\n\nSluggish at 650 km/h and never meant for combat, it nonetheless mounted a single low-powered laser cannon and - unusually for a TIE - a shield generator, plus landing gear and boarding ramps that freed it from Imperial docking racks. Death Squadron used TIE shuttles as physical messengers during the Hoth campaign to avoid comm traffic that would disrupt probe droid transmissions.\n\nOfficers famously disliked it, preferring the luxurious Lambda-class shuttle. In Legends it outlived the Empire: Moff Sarne and the deranged warlord Blitzer Harrsk both met their ends around one, and by 44 ABY a bright-yellow example named Vote Snaplaunce served as an Ithorian politician's campaign vehicle - and, briefly, Luke Skywalker's improvised wind-powered landspeeder.",
  "boarding": "The TIE boarding craft - TIE/br boarding shuttle in Legends - is the assault-transport branch of the TIE bomber family. Sienar Fleet Systems enlarged the port pod into a troop compartment (canon sources say twenty stormtroopers, two full squads) and fitted its nose with a retractable docking claw and military-grade laser cutter for breaching captured or disabled ships. Like the TIE/sh, it carried landing gear and boarding ramps.\n\nCanon gives it surprisingly heavy teeth: paired L-s1 laser cannons, paired H-s1 heavy laser cannons, and warhead launchers; Legends versions instead note a rear laser cannon and the ability to retrieve escape pods. Its most famous sortie bookends Rogue One and A New Hope - Darth Vader used one to cut into the Tantive IV - and the type also appears in Andor.\n\nThe design actually predates the bomber in production terms: a twin-pod Imperial boarding vehicle was drawn for the original 1977 film, cut, then resurrected as the TIE bomber and shuttle for The Empire Strikes Back. In Legends it also served General Sk'ar's kidnapping raid on Shiva IV.",
  "lander": "The TIE lander scales the boarding-shuttle concept up to a full landing craft. Recorded in both canon and Legends (first glimpsed in the 1982 Marvel comic Star Wars 60, later named in Star Wars: Complete Locations), it was a Sienar Fleet Systems troop transport based on the TIE/sa bomber's architecture but with a triple hull and taller, vertically elongated bent solar wings.\n\nWhere the boarding craft carried two squads, the lander deployed an entire stormtrooper company to planetary surfaces - Legends sources call it essentially a scaled-up TIE/br and note the design's kinship with the Imperial Troop Transport. Landers notably equipped the temporary Imperial garrison at Mos Eisley during the hunt for the Death Star plans droids, and figured in Shira Brie's fabricated cover story for infiltrating the Rebel Alliance. Little else about its specifications survives.",
  "itt": "This entry is a fan concept, not an official starship. The image used here is a fan render that grafts TIE bomber-style pods and solar wings onto the boxy silhouette of the Imperial Troop Transport - itself a real design, originating as a 1979 Kenner toy in Legends and canonized as the K79-S80 in Star Wars Rebels.\n\nNo canon or Legends source describes a TIE-derived Imperial Troop Transport, so there is no manufacturer, armament, or service history to report. Its inclusion illustrates a genuine pattern this tree traces elsewhere - the Empire repeatedly stretched the TIE bomber's pod-and-wing architecture into troop carriage, as with the boarding craft and TIE lander - but this particular craft exists only in fan art, and any specifics would be invention.",
  "m4": "The TIE Experimental M4, dubbed the \"TIE Bomb,\" was not a fighter at all but a guided cruise missile built on TIE bones. A Legends craft from the game Star Wars: X-Wing Alliance (circa 3 ABY), it came out of the secret TIE Experimental Project run by Director Lenzer under Grand Admiral Demetrius Zaarin to develop remotely piloted TIE craft.\n\nIt reused wing panels taken directly from the TIE/sa bomber with a similar central pod and struts, but packed the hull with explosives - five times the blast of a kamikaze TIE fighter - and bolted on an oversized booster giving acceleration beyond a TIE Defender's. It carried no crew and no defensive weapons, being steered from a modified Beta-class ETR-3 escort transport.\n\nIts only recorded combat was a retaliatory strike on the Rebel cruiser Liberty, whose fighter screen saved the ship. After the Rebels destroyed the project's research station Obsidian and hangar ship Sardis, no TIE Experimentals were seen again.",
  "gand": "The Gand starfighter is a Legends one-off from Michael Stackpole's X-Wing: The Bacta War - a heavily modified TIE bomber flown by the Gand species, its very name a Wookieepedia conjecture since the novel never labels it formally. Around 7 ABY, three Gand ruetsavii flew these craft alongside Wedge Antilles' ex-Rogues, officially to observe Ooryl Qrygg's worthiness for the honor of janwuine-jika.\n\nThe modifications were comprehensive: the bomb-delivery system in the second hull gave way to a concussion missile launcher fed by a six-missile magazine, while a hyperdrive motivator, navigation computer, and deflector shields - everything the stock bomber lacked - were added. Two extra laser cannons brought the total to four, and the quadanium solar panels were cut down interceptor-style with a central notch for vastly improved peripheral vision.\n\nDespite their ungainly look, the fighters downed their share of TIEs and supported ground-attack missions throughout the Bacta War, a neat illustration of how far the bomber's modular twin-hull could be pushed by someone other than the Empire.",
  "lancet": "The Lancet Aerial Artillery is a Legends atmospheric gunship from Star Wars: Empire at War: Forces of Corruption, built by Sienar Fleet Systems as a deliberate hybrid: the dual cylindrical pods of the TIE/sa bomber merged with the flat-bodied tri-wing configuration of the Lambda-class shuttle, with TIE-style solar arrays worked into the three wings. Game files even call it the \"TIE Lancet,\" though that name never appears in-game.\n\nA repulsorcraft rather than a starfighter, it carried a pilot and gunner and mounted a powerful proton beam cannon on the forward fuselage, angled for air-to-ground attack, backed by two laser cannons for self-defense. It could devastate stationary ground targets but struggled against mobile ones, and - true to its TIE heritage - its lack of shields left it vulnerable to anti-aircraft fire. Little service history exists beyond its Imperial affiliation.",
  "recon_fighter": "The TIE Reconnaissance Fighter, nicknamed \"the Blur\" by its pilots, is a Legends stealth craft from Legacy of the Force: Fury, flying for the Galactic Alliance in 40 ABY - proof the twin-hull TIE layout survived a generation past the Galactic Civil War. It was Sienar Fleet Systems' closest answer to Incom's Jedi StealthX.\n\nOutwardly it kept the classic arrangement of the old bomber, shuttle, and boarding variants: two cylindrical hulls side by side between the wings, cockpit to starboard. The innovation was to portside - where ancestors carried munitions or passengers, the Blur packed a hyperdrive, navigation computer, shield generator, full life support, and sophisticated stealth countermeasures, though its plain black finish fell short of the StealthX's starfield camouflage.\n\nThe prototype served aboard the Star Destroyer Anakin Solo, and Darth Caedus himself flew it to spirit his daughter Allana away from a Jedi ambush - surviving partly because Luke Skywalker held fire upon sensing the child aboard.",
  "crawler": "The TIE crawler, or century tank, answers a question nobody asked: what if a TIE fighter were a tank? Primarily a Legends vehicle (debuting in Dark Empire, 1991, with a brief canon nod in a Choose Your Destiny book), it suspends a TIE/LN-style ball cockpit between two massive tank treads, driven by twin Santhe SSct power generators at up to 90 km/h with a single pilot-gunner.\n\nIt carried the familiar chin-mounted pair of medium blaster cannons plus a retractable light turbolaser slung beneath the cockpit ball - serious firepower for a cheap vehicle. Foot pedals steered while hand controls ran fire control. Like most TIEs it had no shields, trading protection for cost.\n\nIn Legends it was the first new design from the newly independent Santhe/Sienar Technologies after Endor, a mass-produced \"compact assault vehicle\" built from recycled TIE components. It fought in the Imperial Civil War on Coruscant and served the Dark Empire's reconquest, with the TIE ap-1 mauler as its acknowledged forerunner.",
  "mauler": "The TIE ap-1, informally the TIE mauler, is a Legends light ground-assault vehicle from Star Wars: Empire at War, built by Santhe/Sienar Technologies around a standard TIE/LN starfighter cockpit fixed between a pair of tank treads. True to TIE philosophy it emphasized speed - 90 km/h - and cheapness over armor or shields, and was deployed in swarms of five or more to overwhelm defenses.\n\nIt mounted three rapid-fire medium blaster cannons, but crews discovered the treads themselves were often the better weapon: gruesome displays of running down Rebel infantry earned the vehicle its nickname. An enhanced self-destruct device let a cornered pilot overload the engines as a final area-denial measure.\n\nDesigned for the Empire's need for a fast, low-cost ground vehicle early in the Galactic Civil War, it saw limited combat before being phased out in favor of the century tank - the TIE crawler - which Legends sources explicitly describe as its more advanced descendant.",
  "fighter_boat": "The TIE fighter boat is a Legends aquatic oddity from West End Games' adventure Battle for the Golden Sun: an \"aquatic superiority\" TIE designed to operate underwater and skim the surface. Despite the name invoking the TIE/LN, its elongated cockpit was based on the TIE/sa bomber, and it carried a crew of two - pilot and gunner, often aquatic assault stormtroopers - with fire-linked laser cannons.\n\nIts solar wings were remade for the sea: the lower halves bent outward into runners for planing across the water, the upper halves removed entirely for visibility. Fighter boats served Imperial Army aquatic garrisons as patrol and assault craft, working with AT-AT swimmers in deep-water operations, notably from the MFB-4 mobile repulsor base on Alluuvia. No official illustration is widely known - the image used here is a fan render.",
  "subfighter": "The TIE Subfighter is among the most obscure entries here, which is why it appears with a \"?\" placeholder - no reliable visual reference exists. It comes from a single Legends source, the short story \"Death-Hunter\" in Star Wars Adventure Journal 9.\n\nWhat can be said honestly is brief: it was a submersible attack craft in Imperial service, armed with a concussion missile launcher and a single blaster cannon, credited with a speed of 90 km/h. Its hull layout, crew, manufacturer, and relationship to other TIE designs are simply not recorded; even its stub of a Wookieepedia article contains little more. It stands here as evidence that the Empire pushed TIE branding beneath the waves as well as onto treads.",
  "clawcraft": "The Nssis-class Clawcraft sits beside the TIE family tree rather than on it - a Legends hybrid of Imperial and Chiss engineering, associated with the Empire of the Hand and Chiss Ascendancy. It wraps a TIE-series ball cockpit and rear-mounted Sienar ion drive pod in four curving wings that sweep forward like a claw, each tipped with an SFS L-s7.2 laser cannon.\n\nUnlike its TIE progenitors it carried deflector shields, thicker armor, and a class 1.5 hyperdrive, navigating via the Chiss anchor-point beacon network instead of a bulky navicomputer. Slower than a TIE/LN at sublight, it compensated with extreme agility from articulated wings and paired control jets.\n\nIntroduced in Timothy Zahn's Specter of the Past, Clawcraft entered wider galactic view around 25 ABY when Colonel Jagged Fel's Chiss squadrons fought the Yuuzhan Vong at Garqi and Ithor, later flying alongside Rogue and Twin Suns Squadrons. As one Imperial engineer put it, the Chiss did not just steal the technology - they made it better.",
  "aleph": "The Aleph-class starfighter is a Legends heavy fighter that Sienar Fleet Systems built not for the Empire but for the Galactic Alliance - it is included here as only loosely TIE-related, its oversized ball cockpit being a clear descendant of the classic TIE sphere. Conceived late in the Yuuzhan Vong War as the \"Pondskipper,\" a counter to Yuuzhan Vong coralskippers, it reached limited production around 40 ABY, by then nicknamed the \"Twee.\"\n\nEverything about it inverted TIE doctrine: a two-person side-by-side cockpit with an astromech socket, extra-thick armor, powerful shields, quad laser turrets flanking the cockpit like earmuffs, plus proton torpedo and concussion missile tubes. It was fast in a straight line but maneuvered, in Syal Antilles' words, like a plug of duracrete, relying on hull thruster ports for lateral jinks.\n\nAlephs fought through the Second Galactic Civil War - Syal Antilles flew one at Tralus and later defended Jacen Solo from a Jedi StealthX ambush - and still patrolled Coruscant into the 43-44 ABY era.",
  "clutch": "The Clutch is not an Imperial design at all but an \"Ugly\" - one of the improvised starfighters cobbled together from salvaged parts - making it an unofficial cousin of the TIE line. Introduced in Michael Stackpole's novel I, Jedi, it mates a TIE/LN ball cockpit and twin ion engines with three triangular fins set 120 degrees apart, one above the cockpit and two below serving as landing gear.\n\nBuilders cut windows into the ball for visibility and, unlike true TIEs, fitted basic shields. Armament comprised two laser cannons below the cockpit and an ion cannon at the top fin's tip. Quirky controls - pedals for snap turns and pull-out throttle knobs that killed motion instantly - made it surprisingly agile, if slower than an X-wing.\n\nClutches served pirates and irregulars in Legends: Moff Leonia Tavira's Invid pirates flew them, as did privateer Urias Xhaxin during the Yuuzhan Vong War and escorts evacuating Dubrillon. Despite the \"tri-fighter\" nickname, they are unrelated to the Confederacy's droid tri-fighter."
 },
 "edges": {
  "eta2->tie_proto": "This is a loose inheritance rather than a production lineage. Canon's TIE Fighter Owners' Workshop Manual says Raith Sienar, tasked with the Navy's new fighter, was inspired by Kuat Systems Engineering's Eta-2 interceptor, and reference works note the Eta-2 bore the closest resemblance of any predecessor: a central cockpit pod, twin ion engines, and common weapons technology.\n\nWhat changed was philosophy. The Jedi craft's Force-honed agility, ion cannons, and astromech support gave way to a mass-producible, expendable fighter for ordinary Navy pilots. The TIE prototype kept the no-shields, no-hyperdrive austerity and pushed it further, dropping even the transport-ring hyperspace option and swapping exposed radiator S-foils for full solar-collector wings.",
  "vwing->tie_proto": "The same canon source covers this loose link: Raith Sienar drew on the Alpha-3 V-wing alongside the Eta-2 when shaping the TIE line. The debt shows most in silhouette and doctrine — the V-wing contributed the twin vertical wings with folding radiator panels and, critically, its operating model: a compact, hyperdrive-less fighter deployed in swarms from carriers, flown by pilots in sealed life-support suits because an unpressurized cockpit saved mass.\n\nThe TIE prototype regularized all of this into the Imperial pattern, dropping the V-wing's astromech assistance and Kuat manufacture in favor of Sienar's twin ion engines fed by solar-collector wings, and keeping the austere approach that defined every TIE after it.",
  "starcourier->advproject": "This link is speculative, and the sources only partly close the gap. Legends' Essential Guide to Warfare traces the T.I.E. starfighter back to the Sienar courier design that became the Scimitar, and Rogue Planet describes the Advanced Project ship explicitly against the Scimitar — the same compact spherical compartment, minus the bladed forward hull.\n\nThat makes a courier-to-fighter evolution plausible: Sienar shrinking a luxury infiltration transport's core ideas (ball cabin, hot ion engines, large radiator surfaces) into a twenty-meter escort fighter for corporate clients. But no source states outright that the Advanced Project ship was developed from the Star Courier; the shared designer, era, and architecture are the inference being drawn.",
  "advproject->tie_proto": "This step is directly sourced in Legends: Rogue Planet establishes the Advanced Project ship as a prototype of the T.I.E. starfighter, and The Star Wars Sourcebook says the design was expanded into the T.I.E. and its successors.\n\nWhat changed was scale and purpose. The corporate escort — black-painted, twenty meters across, launched from cargo-hold stacks — became a standardized military fighter once Sienar unveiled the SIE-TIE twin ion engine around 22 BBY and the Republic went looking for a cheap fighter it could pack aboard existing warships. The spherical pod and vertical cooling vanes carried over; the mission shifted from freighter escort to fleet service, setting the template for Imperial mass production.",
  "imp_proto->tie_proto": "This edge runs from a comic one-off into the canonical line, and rests on visual reasoning more than explicit text. The Ghost Prison prototype of 19 BBY already has the TIE's spherical solar-winged pod — its wings simply sit horizontally — and Wookieepedia files it among TIE prototypes, but no source names its manufacturer or ties it to Sienar's program.\n\nReading it as a step toward the T.I.E./TIE fighter prototype assumes the Empire's earliest fighter experiments fed one development stream. What visibly changed: the wings rotated to the familiar vertical arrangement, and the terrifyingly unreliable catapult ejection system — prone to vaporizing its pilot — matured into the standard, survivable TIE ejector seat.",
  "tie_proto->tie_sf": "This link is marked speculative, though Legends support is actually fairly strong: The Star Wars Sourcebook calls the TIE starfighter the first upgraded model of the Republic-era T.I.E., produced once the new Empire wanted a mass-production fighter. The hesitation comes from muddled early nomenclature — \"T.I.E. starfighter,\" \"TIE starfighter,\" and the Force Unleashed Campaign Guide's \"TIE Prototype\" overlap heavily, and none of this exists in canon.\n\nTaking the sources at face value, the change was militarization under new management: the design gained a centralized generator running engines and weapons together, weak shields, a small two-jump hyperdrive, and two single-shot concussion missile launchers, producing a tougher, pricier interim fighter for the young Imperial Navy.",
  "tie_sf->tieln": "This is one of the best-documented transitions in the whole tree, and it ran backwards by most measures: the TIE/ln was a cost-cutting simplification, not an upgrade. Legends sources state the Empire stripped the TIE starfighter's secondary energy generators and the systems they powered — shields, the small hyperdrive, missile launchers — to make the fighter drastically cheaper to mass-produce, while splitting propulsion and weapons onto independent generators for reliability.\n\nImproved engines and more powerful laser cannons made the TIE/ln faster and deadlier gun-to-gun despite being less durable. Doctrine drove everything: at roughly 60,000 credits, the Empire could field overwhelming swarms from carrier racks, and by 0 ABY the older model had been effectively replaced or scrapped.",
  "tie_proto->infiltrator": "This link is marked speculative, but it is arguably the least speculative thing about the TIE infiltrator: the craft's single source, the Death Star Owner's Technical Manual, describes it precisely as a Raith Sienar design derived from the T.I.E. fighter.\n\nThe genuine uncertainty is everything else — no date, image, statistics, or armament exist, so what actually changed between parent and derivative cannot be stated. The name suggests a covert or special-mission adaptation of the basic spaceframe, which would make it an early example of the TIE line's habit of spinning role-specific variants off a common chassis, but that reading goes beyond what the one-sentence source can support.",
  "starcourier->adv_prototype": "This edge skips decades but is stated flat-out in canon: Ultimate Star Wars describes the TIE Advanced v1 as heavily based off Republic Sienar Systems' own Scimitar, the modified Star Courier. The inheritance shows in the v1's un-TIE-like refinements — folding wings that stow compactly, a hyperdrive and basic shields befitting an independent long-range operator, and a role built around pursuit and tracking (its launcher fired S-thread trackers) that echoes the Scimitar's espionage outfit.\n\nWhat changed was scale and economy: a one-off Sith infiltration transport's ideas were compressed into a small, produceable fighter for Inquisitors and elite pilots, at about 150,000 credits rather than a bespoke Sith commission.",
  "tieln->adv_prototype": "From the mainline fighter to the v1, nearly every deleted luxury came back. Canon sources describe the v1's engines as an upgrade over the standard TIE/ln powerplant, its chin-mounted laser cannons as stronger than the TIE/ln's, and — most un-TIE-like — the addition of basic deflector shields, a class 4.5 hyperdrive, and folding S-foils that saved hangar space.\n\nArmor moved to the wings' outer faces, with solar panels only inboard, trading collector output for survivability against lateral fire; the engines consequently ran on fuel rather than solar power. The doctrinal shift is the real story: from expendable swarm fighter to a precious experimental machine for Inquisitors, foreshadowing the Empire's grudging turn toward quality.",
  "adv_prototype->advx1": "Canon makes this succession explicit — the Rebel assessment in The Rebel Files calls the x1 \"the next generation of the v1.\" The evolution swapped the v1's folding wings for fixed bent-wing solar arrays with greater collector area, stretched the hull into an elongated rear deck of reinforced durasteel, and kept the breakout features: deflector shields and a hyperdrive (class 4 versus the v1's 4.5).\n\nArmament shifted from the v1's tracker-launcher concept to straightforward killing power — twin heavy L-s9.3 fixed cannons plus a cluster missile launcher. The role narrowed accordingly, from the Inquisitorius's hunting tool to a space-superiority prototype fit for the Emperor's enforcer, and Vader's patronage carried the design into legend.",
  "advx1->sentinel": "Everything about this edge hangs on one sentence and one painting: the TIE Sentinel of Star Wars Galaxies \"possessed traits similar to\" the TIE Advanced x1 and the TIE Aggressor, and was at least planned in the aftermath of Yavin — squarely in the x1's wake.\n\nThe visible changes in the Emperor's Eyes artwork are striking: irregular folded wings recalling the Advanced family's bent arrays, plus at least two dual-gun turrets, one dorsal and one at the chin — a turreted layout alien to single-seat TIE doctrine and closer to the multi-crew Aggressor concept. Nothing about shields, hyperdrive, or cost was ever published, so beyond \"x1-influenced turret fighter\" the developmental details are unrecoverable.",
  "advx1->x1mod": "This is in-family iteration rather than a new type. According to Star Wars Galaxies: Rage of the Wookiees material, Vader had the x1 line extensively modified after Yavin: flaps appeared along the wing edges, and the final iteration reportedly added solar strakes atop the existing panels to pull in more power.\n\nThe purpose of that extra power was an experimental engine/booster combination — addressing the x1's best-known weakness, the sluggishness its shields and hyperdrive imposed on an essentially standard TIE powerplant. The boosters were withheld from ordinary pilots, and the modified airframes went to high-ranking Navy fliers, preserving the x1's character as an elite, limited-issue machine.",
  "advx1->x1var": "This edge covers artistic drift more than engineering. The \"variant\" reflects Legends comic renderings — Marvel's 1977-era Star Wars issues put Advanced-style bent-wing fighters in ordinary squadron service, as in the Imperial attack on the Wheel, drawn with proportions and details that stray from the screen model — and Geoffrey Mandel's 1978 blueprints show similar \"TIE Assault Craft\" that Wookieepedia suggests may be renamed x1s or a sister design.\n\nRead generously, the changes amount to a service version of Vader's prototype: same bent wings and pod, deployed in numbers the films never showed. Read strictly, nothing changed at all — these are period drawings of the x1, and they are treated as a distinct node here for completeness.",
  "advx1->avenger": "Legends states this succession directly: the TIE Avenger was the culmination of the Advanced series begun with Vader's x1. The x1 had pioneered fighter shields and a hyperdrive but paid in maneuverability and cost; the Avenger solved the propulsion half with new P-sx7.4 engines (133 MGLT, out-turning even A-wings) and the hyperdrive half with the compact, Habeen-developed ND9 motivator.\n\nFirepower doubled to four wingtip L-s9.3 cannons plus two general-purpose warhead launchers, with an optional tractor beam — a first. Doctrine shifted with it: the Avenger marked the Empire's deliberate break from quantity-over-quality, though at 400,000 credits production stayed limited. Canon compresses this into the Andor-era TIE Avenger prototype inspired by post-x1 development.",
  "advx1->advx2": "This link is marked speculative, though the Legends source does say the TIE/x2 incorporated many of the design innovations from the TIE Advanced x1 — engines outperforming the TIE/ln's, a hyperdrive, and improved combat software. The caution is fair because the x-numbered testbeds are known only from a newsnet blurb in the Adventure Journal, which never states the x2 was developed from the x1 rather than alongside it.\n\nWhat visibly changed: the x2 abandoned the x1's spaceframe for an even more experimental hull and introduced inward-swept solar/radiator panels to fix the TIE line's notorious visibility problem — a pilot-centered refinement the x1 had never attempted.",
  "advx2->advx3": "This link is marked speculative for the same reason: the x3 is attested only alongside the x2 in a Galaxywide NewsNets item, which says it incorporated innovations from both the x1 and x2 without spelling out a development chain.\n\nAssuming the sequence, the x3 was a deliberate step backward. It kept the improved ion engines, combat software, and inward-swept visibility panels, but returned to a conventional TIE fighter hullform and deleted the hyperdrive the x1 and x2 carried. That pattern looks like Sienar isolating variables — testing which advanced features justified their cost — and it foreshadows the Navy's persistent refusal to pay for hyperdrives in mainline fighters.",
  "advx3->advx7": "No source documents an x4 through x6, so this edge jumps from the last visibility testbed straight to the Defender's direct precursor on the strength of the shared x-series designation. The change is dramatic: the x3 was a conservative, hyperdrive-less testbed with standard paired wings, while the TIE Advanced x7 was, per Legends, a radical departure — three solar collectors spaced evenly around the cockpit pod, triple maneuvering-jet arrays, four fire-linked laser cannons, and speed well beyond standard TIEs.\n\nThe tri-wing geometry that would define the Defender appears here first. Canon agrees on the role, calling the x7 the prototype from which the earliest TIE/D Defenders developed, with six lost gathering combat data at Hoth.",
  "advx7->defender": "Both continuities agree the Defender came directly from the x7, and the changes are unusually well specified. Legends says the x7 prototype lacked the ion cannons, missile launchers, and hyperdrive that made it into the finished fighter; the production TIE/d added exactly those — two NK-3 ion cannons, twin warhead launchers, and a class-2 hyperdrive — plus Novaldex forward and rear shields and an optional tractor beam, turning a fast tri-wing testbed into a true multi-role fighter-bomber.\n\nCanon frames the same step through Thrawn's Lothal program, with Build the Millennium Falcon crediting Hoth-era x7 data. The cost of completion was fierce: roughly 300,000 credits per unit, which did as much as any rebel to kill the program.",
  "defender->redstar1": "This final edge is customization, not development: Red Star 1 was a single TIE/D Defender tailored to its owner, Countess Iran Ryad of Red Star Squadron. Over the stock fighter she added an improved deflector shield generator, an upgraded ion engine, and an enhanced fire-control system, wrapped in a red paint scheme — refinements that played to the Defender's existing strengths rather than changing its role.\n\nIts listed 262,500-credit value sits oddly against the sources (the same article prices a standard Defender at 200,000, others at 300,000), a contradiction Wookieepedia flags. The ship's end was characteristically Imperial drama: charged with treason around 5 ABY, Ryad was hunted down and destroyed by Baron Soontir Fel.",
  "advx1->interceptor": "The conventional account, in both continuities, runs the Interceptor's ancestry through Darth Vader's TIE Advanced x1: Sienar took the Advanced's bent-wing solar panel concept, sharpened it into full dagger wings, moved the firepower to four wingtip cannons, and stripped out everything expensive — the hyperdrive, shields, and heavier armor that made the x1 impractical to mass-produce. The result kept the Advanced's performance pedigree at a price the Navy could buy by the thousand.\n\nThis link is flagged uncertain, and its origin is ambiguous. One argument holds the Interceptor might better descend from the x3 test variant precisely because the Interceptor lacks a hyperdrive: deriving a hyperdrive-less fighter from a hyperdrive-less experimental airframe is arguably a cleaner lineage than from the hyperdrive-equipped x1. Published sources, however, consistently cite the Advanced x1 line and related bent-wing prototypes such as the TIE/ad v1.",
  "interceptor->royal_guard": "This is a straight upgrade path: take a stock TIE Interceptor and, under Darth Vader's supervision, add everything the line normally denies its pilots. The Royal Guard variant gained a built-in hyperdrive and shield generator, extra flaps on the wing curves, and the Guard's crimson livery — and, remarkably, came out faster and more maneuverable than the fleet model, whereas ordinary Interceptors retrofitted with similar gear (like Apwar Trigit's) paid for it in sluggishness. The role narrowed too: from mass-produced space superiority fighter to bespoke escort for Palpatine's shuttle and its decoys, flown only by Royal Guardsmen whose skills reputedly rivaled the 181st and Rogue Squadron. Cost effectively became irrelevant — these were commissioned by the Emperor and never offered for sale.",
  "interceptor->crimson": "The visible change here is paint and purpose: standard TIE/IN interceptors finished in the crimson of the Emperor's Royal Guard, deployed in canon as close escort for the shuttle carrying Palpatine and Vader at the Battle at Amaxine Station. Sources record no mechanical differences at all — no confirmed shields, hyperdrive, or armament changes — so on the evidence this may be a role-and-livery variant rather than a re-engineering.\n\nIts real significance is continuity: it is the canon echo of Legends' upgraded Royal Guard TIE Interceptor, occupying the same bodyguard niche, and Wookieepedia formally pairs the two articles as counterparts. Whether canon's version conceals similar upgrades under the red paint simply has not been established.",
  "interceptor->m3": "The M3 shows how far the Interceptor airframe could be bent: Zaarin's TIE Experimental Project deleted the laser cannons entirely and hung two warhead launchers on the outer wing hubs, feeding eight concussion missiles — turning a gun-armed dogfighter into a dedicated missile bomber. Most airframes also gained a 30 SBD shield generator with no discernible performance loss, though speed and maneuverability dipped slightly from the donor craft.\n\nThe most radical change was removing the pilot: M3s were drone fighters, slaved to a modified Beta-class ETR-3 escort transport with no onboard navigation of their own. That made them expendable but brittle — once the Rebels learned to disable the control ships, whole formations went inert, and the type died with the project's facilities around 3 ABY.",
  "interceptor->starhunter": "Legacy of the Force: Invincible states outright that the Starhunter was an evolution of the TIE Interceptor, so the direction of this edge is textually grounded; what earns the uncertainty flag is that this single line is nearly all we have. The visible changes: a deflector shield generator became standard, and unspecified heavy weapons joined the laser cannons, giving the Imperial Remnant of 41 ABY an interceptor that could finally absorb a hit and threaten larger targets. Whether it kept the classic wing geometry, gained a hyperdrive, or was even a Sienar product is unrecorded — so the arrow marks a stated descent whose actual engineering content is mostly blank, and any detailed picture of the transition would be invention.",
  "starhunter->predator": "This edge bridges a long gap from the Remnant's shielded interceptor evolution to the Fel Empire's standard fighter, and it tracks the interceptor line's steady accretion of capability. Where the Starhunter added shields and heavy weapons, the Predator completed the package: a Class 1 hyperdrive and navicomputer for independent operations, shields generated through articulated blade-shaped wings that adjusted in flight and folded for docking, and four L-s9.3 laser cannons.\n\nLegends sources describe the Predator as the TIE Interceptor's successor generally, without naming the Starhunter as an intermediate step, so the Starhunter serves here as a chronological waypoint — reasonable, since both are explicitly interceptor-line descendants in Remnant and Fel Empire service. The cost of maturity was complexity: the wing systems were a maintenance nightmare, and a new Predator ran 250,000 credits.",
  "predator->neutralizer": "Sienar engineers built the Neutralizer by adapting the Predator's design into a dedicated bomber: the airframe grew larger and bulkier, agility dropped, and the four-cannon fighter armament gave way to two medium laser cannons plus a launcher with ten proton torpedoes. It kept the Class 1 hyperdrive and navicomputer and carried unusually strong shields for its size, while the role shifted wholly to strafing ground emplacements and cracking fortified positions — later including Krayt's punitive air strikes on suspected Fel loyalists.\n\nThere is a dating quirk here: the Predator is usually dated to 130 ABY, yet the officially Predator-derived Neutralizer was in action from the Sith–Imperial War beginning 127 ABY. The wrinkle dissolves once 130 ABY is read as when Predators first appear on-panel (Legacy #1), not when they entered service — sources have Predators as the Fel Empire's primary fighter throughout that same war.",
  "predator->sith_imp": "This succession is attested but almost entirely opaque. In 137 ABY Darth Rauder ordered that Skull Squadron trade its TIE Predators for the new Sith-Imperial starfighters, billed as 'the latest design and a symbol of the Sith Empire' — so the edge represents a deliberate frontline replacement within Krayt's forces rather than a gradual evolution. Visually the newcomer kept the TIE family resemblance while leaning harder into the Clawcraft-like silhouette with a quad-wing arrangement.\n\nBeyond laser cannons and a single pilot, no specifications were ever published, so what actually improved — speed, shields, sensors — is unknown. The change we can document is political as much as technical: a Sith regime stamping its own identity onto the century-old Imperial fighter line.",
  "defender->yards": "This loose edge is a framing device, flagged as speculation: it feeds the TIE Defender into the Imperial Classified Flight Yards, the testing center where Soontir Fel evaluated a family of advanced TIEs in 0 BBY. The kernel of source support comes from the Legends sourcebook Stay on Target, which places development of the Aggressor, Hunter, Defender, and Phantom under then-Captain Fel's oversight at the Classified Flight Yards.\n\nThe speculative leap is treating the Defender as the program's technological wellspring. It is plausible reasoning: the Defender embodied exactly the heresies the program explored — shields, hyperdrives, tri-wing geometry, and survivability-first design in place of cheap expendability — but no source states that the Defender directly parented the other craft.",
  "yards->hunter": "What the Hunter drew from the Defender-era testing program was its break with TIE austerity: like the Defender, it shipped with both deflector shields and a hyperdrive, and it was field-tested by Soontir Fel himself, tying it directly to the Classified Flight Yards program. Its distinct contribution was studying the enemy rather than the lineage — its S-foils deliberately copied the X-wing it was built to kill, its wing layout roughly inverted the Interceptor's, and its two lasers, two ion cannons, and twelve proton torpedoes matched Rebel multi-role loadouts almost bomber-for-bomber. The trade-off was exclusivity: costly at up to 275,000 credits and slower to produce than the Navy wanted, it went only to the storm commandos instead of the general fleet.",
  "yards->aggressor": "The Aggressor is the best-documented member of this cluster — Stay on Target explicitly says its development was overseen at the Imperial Classified Flight Yards by Captain Fel alongside the Hunter, Defender, and Phantom — yet it took the opposite lesson from the program. Rather than shields and hyperdrives, it explored crew specialization: an elongated command pod added a dedicated gunner working a dorsal twin light laser turret, backed by forward medium lasers and ordnance, while keeping the shieldless, hyperdrive-less TIE formula.\n\nIts role diverged too, from dogfighting toward commerce raiding, interdiction, patrol, and surveillance. Reliable and deadly but expensive to crew and train for, it never fully left the experimental phase — a fittingly provisional fate for a Flight Yards testbed.",
  "yards->phantom": "The Phantom reads naturally as the testing program's stealth branch. Stay on Target places its development oversight with Soontir Fel at the Classified Flight Yards, and it shares the Defender's defining departures — deflector shields, a Class 1 hyperdrive, and a tri-wing solar array layout — grafted onto the V38 airframe rather than a standard TIE pod.\n\nWhat the program added on top was unprecedented: Grand Admiral Batch's stygium cloaking device, an automated decloak-fire-recloak system, a two-man crew with full life support, and a self-destruct to keep the secret. At 365,000 credits per unit it pushed survivability-over-cost philosophy to its limit, and like the other Yards craft it never reached mass service.",
  "yards->reaper": "This edge is flagged uncertain, and rightly so: no published source connects the TIE Reaper to the Classified Flight Yards or to Soontir Fel's testing program, which is Legends material, while the Reaper is a canon craft. The reasoning is plausible on features alone — the Reaper carries deflector shields, a Class 1 hyperdrive, life support, and ejector seats, exactly the Defender-era luxuries ordinary TIEs were denied, here applied to a two-crew, ten-trooper attack lander.\n\nChronology complicates it further: canon shows a Reaper in Inquisitorial service by 14 BBY, well before the 0 BBY testing window. Best read, the arrow claims the Reaper as kin to the shielded, hyperdrive-equipped experimental TIE family rather than as a literal product of the Yards.",
  "yards->striker": "Another explicitly speculative edge. Canon actually gives the striker a different origin — a design that emerged from the Empire's Scarif think tank, flying by the 18 BBY Empire Day parade — which sits awkwardly beside a 0 BBY Defender-era testing program. What makes the association tempting is the craft's designation and character: 'TIE/sk x1 experimental air superiority fighter' marks it as a formally experimental type that was never a favorite of the Admiralty, and it shares its streamlined, flat-winged airframe family with the Reaper, the other uncertain Yards edge.\n\nUnlike the Hunter or Phantom, though, the striker kept pure TIE economics — no shields, no hyperdrive, a 50,000-credit price — so its claimed link to the program rests on experimental status and airframe kinship, not equipment.",
  "dagger->v38": "This edge reaches back before the Empire existed: the Dagger-class was a Republic Sienar Systems product of the Naboo era, and Legends sources state its design was later used as the basis for the V38 assault fighter. The inheritance is geometric — the Dagger's three knife-like wings arranged around a central cockpit reappear in the V38's triple-wing layout, with panels mounted directly to the hull.\n\nThe changes are generational: a cheap 35,000-credit mercenary fighter with a Class 2 hyperdrive, three lasers, and no life support became an Imperial development airframe built on Imdaar, its wings now quadanium solar collectors in TIE fashion and its cockpit a non-spherical pod closer to the Scimitar bomber. In effect, Sienar's successor company mined its own back catalog for a tri-wing testbed.",
  "v38->phantom": "The Phantom is literally a modified V38 — early sources even call it the 'Phantom V38' — making this the tree's most direct edge. Grand Admiral Batch's Imdaar Alpha project took the 'failed' assault fighter and packed its armored triangular hull with the systems that defined the finished craft: a Mk. III stygium cloaking device, deflector shields, a Class 1 hyperdrive and navicomputer, and the generators to feed them.\n\nArmament settled at five laser cannons — three wingtip, two under the cockpit — worked by a two-man crew with full life support, and every airframe gained a remotely triggered self-destruct. The role transformed accordingly, from unremarkable assault fighter to strategic stealth weapon meant to gut the Rebel fleet, at a staggering 365,000 credits apiece.",
  "tieln->m1": "The M1 is the most radical surgery on the TIE/LN in this tree: engineers kept the standard cockpit pod and wing panel as building blocks but reassembled them into an asymmetric twin-pod craft, one wing panel braced between two pods. The pilot was removed entirely, control systems filling the port pod, and a capital-grade turbolaser filling the starboard one, firepower no crewed TIE/LN could carry.\n\nDoctrinally the change was even bigger than the hardware: the M1 flew by remote control from a Beta-class escort transport, with a hyperdrive slaved to the controller. The Empire was using the cheap, proven TIE/LN as raw material for its first serious step toward expendable drone fighters, trading pilot survivability concerns for heavy armament and disposability.",
  "tieln->m2": "Of the TIE Experimental Project craft, the M2 changed the TIE/LN least structurally and most in armament philosophy. The airframe, wings, and engines stayed essentially stock; the chin-mounted twin laser cannons were deleted and replaced by two turbolaser turrets mounted on the outer wing hubs, giving a snub fighter capital-ship-class guns. Optional light shielding (about 20 SBD) addressed the TIE's proverbial fragility.\n\nAs with its siblings, the pilot came out of the loop: the M2 was flown remotely from a modified escort transport while computers recorded human piloting behavior for future droid-brain fighters. Speed matched the TIE/LN with slightly worse handling, the price of hauling turbolasers on a fighter reactor. The role shifted accordingly, from space superiority to experimental heavy assault against convoys and warships.",
  "tieln->m5": "The M5 asked a simple question: how fast can a TIE/LN go? The answer was a large booster engine grafted onto the rear of the ball cockpit, stretching the craft to ten meters and pushing it to 132 MGLT, dramatically faster than the standard fighter and beyond even the TIE Interceptor, at a small cost in maneuverability. Armament stayed the familiar two laser cannons.\n\nLike the rest of the Experimental Project line it was remotely piloted from an escort transport, carried a slaved hyperdrive for controlled deployment, and frequently added shield generators with no measured performance loss. The M5 thus represents the TIE/LN adapted as a high-speed interception and harassment drone, pure pursuit performance layered on an unchanged weapons fit.",
  "tieln->oppressor": "The Oppressor took the TIE/LN concept in the opposite direction from most variants: instead of stripping weight, Sienar piled it on. The goal was a strike fighter and light bomber, a TIE-family answer to the Y-wing, so the spaceframe grew bulkier, gaining heavy armor, shields, expanded solar arrays for power, and a potent mixed armament of two L-s9.3 laser cannons plus missile launchers and projectile mounts.\n\nThe trade was classic: it retained the same basic engine system as earlier TIEs under far more mass, so the hallmark TIE agility dropped to merely average, though it stayed nimble for a bomber. Role and doctrine changed too, from expendable swarm fighter to expensive vanguard assault craft, entrusted only to veteran pilots under a limited fielding-test program rather than mass-issued.",
  "tieln->automated": "The TIE/D was the logical endpoint of the drone experiments: Sienar engineers explicitly based it on the TIE/LN, and the transformation removed the pilot altogether. In place of a human sat a Cybot Galactica Ace-6 droid brain; the mass saved on life support and pilot accommodations went into heavier armor and shielding. The classic radiator wings became rectangular panels with adjustable pitch, and despite the added protection the TIE/D was faster in atmosphere than a TIE Interceptor.\n\nDoctrine changed as much as hardware. Where TIE/LNs spent cheap pilots, the Dark Empire spent cheap airframes: World Devastator factories extruded swarms of TIE/Ds from battlefield salvage, giving squadrons that never fatigued, needed minimal maintenance, and were perfectly loyal. The weakness was the same one the Clone Wars exposed, droid brains updated with new tactics were still routinely outwitted by living pilots.",
  "vulture->automated": "This is a loose conceptual link rather than an engineering lineage: Clone Wars-era droid starfighters were the basis for the TIE/D's operating concept, not its airframe. Legends sources make the connection explicit. When General Arndall Lott's remote-slaving experiments proved inefficient and jammable, Imperial scientists turned to the Separatists' droid fighters as the model for autonomous combat craft, and Director Lenzer's earlier TIE Experimental Project aimed to build droid fighters 'many times more lethal' than the Vultures.\n\nWhat carried over was doctrine: expendable, mass-produced, hyperdrive-less fighters winning by swarm rather than skill, with an onboard droid brain replacing the vulnerable central control ship the Trade Federation used at Naboo. What did not carry over was the hardware, since the TIE/D's pod, engines, and cannons all came from the TIE/LN.",
  "tieln->raptor": "The Raptor is a TIE/LN reimagined by someone outside Sienar Fleet Systems entirely: Warlord Zsinj's engineers at Dathomir, working after Endor with TIE components but no corporate blueprint discipline. The signature change was amputating the big solar wing panels and replacing them with four short fins set at right angles around the ball cockpit, yielding a smaller, harder-to-hit fighter that matched an A-wing for maneuverability, though it gave up the Interceptor's top speed.\n\nArmament jumped from the TIE/LN's two lasers to four laser cannons plus two concussion missile launchers, and some prototypes added shields, addressing the standard TIE's two classic weaknesses in one redesign. The role shifted from mass-issue space superiority to a warlord faction's premium fighter, and it never entered mainline Imperial Navy service.",
  "tieln->vigilant": "This edge is best treated as visual rather than historical. The 'Vigilant' derives from Kenner's TIE fighter toys of the Return of the Jedi era, whose sculpts compressed and simplified the TIE/LN, notably in wing panel size and proportions, for shelf-friendly production. Fans have retroactively read that toy geometry as a distinct short-winged variant of the standard fighter.\n\nSo what 'changed' from the TIE/LN is essentially an artifact of merchandising: a stubbier, chunkier interpretation of the same fighter, with no documented differences in armament, role, or doctrine because no story material exists. Anything more specific would be invention; the edge records a design relationship between a real fighter and its toy-derived echo.",
  "tieln->mining_guild": "This link is flagged uncertain, but here canon actually confirms the derivation: dialogue in Rebels and the Databank describe the TIE/mg as a modified TIE/ln supplied to the Mining Guild. The uncertainty is only about where it branches in this tidy lineage, not whether it branches.\n\nThe changes trace a military fighter demoted to corporate tool. A notch cut from each wing left eight solar collectors instead of twelve, improving the pilot's sightlines for survey work while degrading power and maneuverability; yellow livery distinguished Guild craft from Imperial Navy TIEs. The Guild added basic shields, protecting hardware and trained pilots a corporation cannot treat as expendable, plus specialized sensors for locating valuable ores and gases. Role shifted from space superiority to resource prospecting and anti-theft patrol.",
  "tieln->sentry": "This edge is flagged uncertain, and rightly so: the 'TIE Sentry' rests on artwork in the manga adaptation of A New Hope, which depicts TIE-type craft in a static guard posture. The speculation is that these represent a TIE/LN adapted for sentry duty, and the reasoning is plausible — the Empire demonstrably converted the baseline fighter for niche roles, and picket duty around installations like the Death Star is exactly the kind of job a cheap, short-ranged TIE would draw.\n\nBut no source establishes what, if anything, actually changed from the standard fighter: no stats, no modifications, no designation. It is equally possible the manga simply drew ordinary TIE fighters with artistic license. The honest summary is a hypothetical role-variant with zero documented differences.",
  "tieln->iden": "This edge is flagged uncertain because it connects a production fighter to a single named airframe rather than a variant. Iden Versio's ship is, by name and appearance, a stock TIE/ln space superiority starfighter; what Battlefront II changes is everything around it. As a hero ship her TIE handles better and hits harder than the standard fighter, with abilities like afterburner sprints and torpedo salvos.\n\nThe plausible reading is that this represents an elite pilot's individually maintained and tuned machine, the way ace units historically got the best-fettled examples of a common type, rather than a formal Sienar modification program. Nothing in canon documents specific hardware differences, so the edge marks distinguished use of the TIE/LN, not derivation from it.",
  "tieln->light_duty": "Uniquely in this tree, the arrow points downhill: the TIE Light Duty is the TIE/LN made deliberately worse. Airframes were typically reconditioned standard TIEs rebuilt for training service, with inferior components, lighter armor, and reduced firepower from its two L-s1 cannons. Limiters were fitted to the solar ionization reactor, with a secondary generator carrying the weapons load.\n\nThe rationale reshaped the role entirely, from frontline space superiority to flight instruction. Detuning meant a ham-fisted novice could neither do serious damage with the fighter nor to it, sparing the fragile reactor and superstructure from student abuse. Doctrinally it acknowledges something the Empire rarely admitted about the TIE line: the standard fighter was unforgiving enough that rookies needed a defanged version first.",
  "tieln->super_tieln": "The Super TIE is the TIE/LN with its known deficiencies patched by people who had time on their hands: scientists at the isolated Maw Installation, who began disassembling and rebuilding the facility's TIE complement as a hobby. The visible change was minimal, a slightly enlarged engine nacelle, but inside they fitted a more powerful engine and markedly better targeting systems, producing a faster, deadlier fighter while keeping the standard two-cannon armament.\n\nThe role shifted from general space superiority to point-defense interception, seizing or destroying any ship that strayed near the secret installation. Doctrinally it is a footnote with a moral: developed in isolation from mainstream Imperial R&D, the Super TIE solved the same problems the TIE Interceptor solved better, showing how the Empire's compartmentalization duplicated effort.",
  "tieln->fire_control": "The TIE/fc conversion is a clean one-for-one trade: one of the TIE/LN's two laser cannons came out, and a suite of sensors went in. Firepower halved to a single gun; in exchange the fighter could scan enemy bases and formations in detail and transmit precise targeting data back to the fleet, letting warships strike accurately while evading defenses.\n\nThe role change is total, from shooter to spotter. Imperial doctrine paired it with heavier platforms, most explicitly the TIE/gt bomber, which was designed to depend on TIE/fc target designation for precision work, though large fixed structures could be hit without it. Because the craft was operationally precious and nearly unarmed, engineers kept it highly maneuverable, its survival depending on agility rather than the guns it gave up.",
  "tieln->brute": "The Brute began as a straightforward up-gunning attempt that failed: Sienar engineers found the standard TIE airframe simply could not handle heavy H-s9.3 twin laser cannons. Their solution defined the variant, an outrigger second pod slung beside the cockpit ball carrying the guns on a self-powered pivot mount, plus an MGK-300 integrated droid brain providing astromech-style flight assistance.\n\nAlmost everything else changed in the direction of survivability, against TIE orthodoxy: triple-laminate quadranium-reinforced armor, extra-strong transparisteel viewport, life support with a pressurized cockpit, even an ejector seat. Speed fell to 90 MGLT and some admirals grumbled that a self-sufficient heavy fighter undercut the case for support vessels. The role shifted from expendable swarm fighter to flight-leader and heavy-hitter, the fighter Han Solo flew as a cadet and dueled in the Maelstrom.",
  "tieln->stealth": "This edge is marked uncertain, but the source novel describes the TIE stealth outright as a modified TIE/ln, so the derivation itself is solid; only the details are thin. The modifications served one purpose, not being seen: a secondary set of smaller low-emission twin ion engines for quiet approach, a sensor-dampening hull coating (possibly reflec), landing gear for autonomous ground operations, and possibly enhanced sensors, given one threaded the gap between the Jedi Praxeum's shield and the Yavin 4 treetops.\n\nThe role inverted the TIE/LN's whole philosophy. Instead of swarming loudly from carriers, the stealth inserted single Imperial commandos deep behind defenses, effectively a fighter-sized infiltration transport. Notably, the conversion sacrificed little combat ability; flown by Jaina Solo it still downed several enemy fighters.",
  "tieln->scoutship": "On the evidence of the Heir to the Empire comic, almost nothing visible changed: Thrawn's scout ship looks like a standard TIE/LN. The one transformative addition is a hyperdrive, which quietly removes the TIE line's defining operational constraint, total dependence on a carrier or base. That single change converts a short-legged point-defense fighter into an independent reconnaissance and infiltration asset, demonstrated when Thrawn's operatives used them for the information raid on Obroa-skai's Celebratus Archive.\n\nEverything else is uncertain: armament, sensors, and even its formal designation are unrecorded, and the craft's identity as a distinct model rests on visual comparison. The suggestion that it might be a later, hyperdrive-equipped evolution of the TIE/rc is reasonable speculation, a recon variant gaining strategic range, but no source confirms it.",
  "tieln->tie_recon": "The TIE/rc is the classic pattern for this whole branch of the tree: subtract a gun, add eyes. One of the TIE/LN's two laser cannons was replaced with additional sensor and communications equipment, leaving a single L-s1 cannon, while upgraded thrusters made the recon bird even more maneuverable than the already-agile baseline fighter.\n\nThe mission changed from fighting to seeing. TIE/rcs ranged ahead of fleets and fighter wings, relaying pre-engagement reconnaissance through their improved comm gear, and worked cluttered environments like asteroid fields where capital ships could not follow. Because it kept the TIE line's lack of hyperdrive and shields, doctrine adapted around the limitation: carriers dropped TIE/rc squadrons into a system and jumped clear, the scouts sweeping through and meeting the carrier at a rendezvous elsewhere.",
  "tie_recon->vanguard": "The vanguard is an incremental, second-generation refinement of the TIE/rc rather than a new concept. The recon fit deepened: large antennae for sensor arrays and communications equipment sprouted from the cockpit ball, and the upper sections of the quadanium solar panels were shortened and swept outward specifically to stop the wings interfering with the dorsal scanners, making it visibly more distinct from a standard TIE than its predecessor was.\n\nThe other addition answered the TIE/rc's key operational flaw. A scout that dies before reporting is worthless, so the vanguard carried limited deflector shields, unusual for a TIE, valued as much for protecting gathered intelligence as the pilot. Armament stayed a single chin cannon, and the mission stayed sublight pre-battle reconnaissance of enemy fleet movements, in service by the Battle of Yavin.",
  "tieln->tie_scout": "This is drawn as a loose, uncertain edge, and that caution is warranted. The Legends TIE/sr shares the TIE family's engines, cockpit design language, and naming, but at 24 meters with an elongated multi-crew fuselage, bent wings, a navigation computer, and the best hyperdrive of any TIE, it is less a modified TIE/LN than a parallel design built from the same technology. Legends sources even have its civilian sibling, the Lone Scout-A, pioneering twin ion engine technology under Republic Sienar Systems, suggesting the scout lineage may predate or run alongside the fighter rather than descend from it.\n\nThe reasoning is still plausible: within the TIE series, the /sr fills the logical endpoint of the recon branch, trading the fighter's agility and cheapness for strategic range, endurance, and com-scan capability. The canon TIE scout of The Rise of Skywalker, a hyperdrive-equipped reconnaissance TIE, fits the same conceptual slot.",
  "tie_scout->lone_scout": "The Lone Scout-A is the TIE/sr translated for the open market, and per Legends sources the civilian variant of the design. The military scout's restricted-issue fittings gave way to exploration gear: passenger space for three, a 150-metric-ton cargo hold, a full year of consumables against the /sr's six months, and a Class 2 hyperdrive with a Class 15 backup, redundancy a civilian alone in the wild regions needs. It even gained deflector shields, which the military TIE/sr lacked, though its sensor array, while high quality, was less sensitive than the military suite.\n\nArmament stayed nominal at a single laser cannon. The deeper change was who flew it: unlike any true TIE it was sold openly to civilian scouts, while also serving the Imperial Survey Corps and later the New Republic, shifting the role from fleet reconnaissance to frontier exploration.",
  "tieln->outland": "This edge is flagged uncertain, but canon supports it directly: the Databank describes standard TIEs being 'adjusted' into Outland fighters as the Empire lost territory. The uncertainty, if any, is whether it was a conversion program or new production, which sources leave vague.\n\nWhat changed is entirely about basing, not fighting. The TIE/ln assumed intact Imperial infrastructure, hangar rack armatures, carriers, prepared pads; the collapsing Empire could assume none of it. So the Outland's solar wings fold at mid-span into a V for landing, and tripodal landing gear recessed in the hull lets it set down on uneven wilderness terrain with easy cockpit access. Armament (two laser cannons) and flight performance stayed essentially TIE/ln-standard. It is doctrine embodied in hardware: a space superiority fighter reworked for a guerrilla-era Imperial remnant operating from nowhere in particular.",
  "tieln->gable": "This edge is flagged uncertain, and it represents a customized individual aircraft rather than a variant: Admiral Gable Karius' fighter in Vader Immortal is identified as a modified TIE/ln space superiority starfighter, so the derivation is sourced even if the details are sparse. The one documented change is an extra turret mounted on the craft's left side, giving the admiral's personal machine firepower and firing arcs beyond the fixed chin cannons of a stock TIE/LN.\n\nNo further specifications exist; the character appears in the Vader Immortal VR series, defending Fortress Vader on Mustafar. The reasonable reading of the edge: senior officers could and did fly one-off, up-armed TIE/LN customs, and Karius' turret-fitted fighter, destroyed in the Fortress Vader hangar battle, is a documented example.",
  "tieln->ground_target": "The step from TIE/LN to TIE/gt is the moment the TIE became a bomber. The spherical ball cockpit was elongated into a cylindrical pod - the first TIE to make that change - and the hull enlarged and reinforced to enclose an internal ordnance bay. Armament shifted accordingly: the fighter's paired chin lasers gave way to a single laser cannon plus a modular concussion missile launcher handling missiles, torpedoes, mines, cluster bombs, or propaganda leaflets. Crew stayed at one pilot and the hexagonal wings were retained, but the role changed completely, from space superiority to ground-targeting and support, with TIE/fc craft supplying precision targeting data the gt lacked on its own.",
  "ground_target->sa_bomber": "From gt to sa, the single stretched hull split into two: the TIE bomber's signature twin-pod layout put the pilot in a starboard cockpit and moved all ordnance into a dedicated portside bay with its own bomb chute, targeting computer, and loading arm. Payload flexibility and volume jumped - proton torpedoes, concussion missiles, proton bombs, orbital mines, thermal detonators, or a passenger cabin - and fixed armament doubled to two laser cannons. The wings grew into larger bent panels with greater solar-collection area to feed the heavier craft, and the pilot gained an ejector seat and life support. Role-wise the mission stayed the same - surface assault and precision bombing - but the sa did it so much better that it purged the gt from frontline squadrons.",
  "sa_bomber->heavy_bomber": "The Heavy Bomber's change is simple and additive: where the TIE/sa carried one ordnance module beside the cockpit, the heavy variant carried dual weapons pods - described in Legends material as the second pod stacked atop the first - roughly doubling deliverable ordnance while keeping wings and command module nearly identical to the standard bomber. Crew and cockpit arrangement appear unchanged. The role shifted toward ground pounding: in Galactic Battlegrounds, its only source, the craft excels against tanks, artillery, and enemy power cores rather than shipping. It is best understood as the transitional experiment proving the TIE bomber's modular hull could take on extra pods, the idea the TIE Punisher would push to four.",
  "heavy_bomber->interdictor": "The Punisher completes the pod-stacking logic the Heavy Bomber began: wings and command module stay nearly identical to the TIE bomber and Heavy Bomber - Legends sources make that comparison explicitly - but the command module is now flanked on both sides by paired ordnance pods, four in all, with twin linked bomb bays and launcher magazines for around four times a TIE bomber's payload. The bigger change is survivability doctrine: deflector shields, heavier armor, and enhanced sensor scopes appear, because a bomber that expensive must live long enough to deliver. Crew remains a single pilot, and the role becomes long-endurance heavy bombardment where Star Destroyers cannot go. Both craft debuted in Galactic Battlegrounds, which makes this the most natural developmental pairing here.",
  "heavy_bomber->terror": "This arrow rests on a visual observation Wookieepedia itself makes: the unnamed Terror Project TIE of The Force Unleashed II comic used a triple-fuselage arrangement \"nearly identical\" to the TIE Heavy Bomber's, minus the ventral ordnance pod. The changes repurpose the bomber into an assault-insertion platform. The concave bomber wings became outward-canted panels recalling the TIE Advanced or Defender, with wingtip weapons observed firing ion cannons - disabling tools, not demolition ones. The port-side pods traded bombs for large projectile launchers firing boarding pods packed with terror troopers and battle droids, and a hyperdrive gave it independent reach. Ordnance-hauler became trooper-delivery weapon; note that both the \"TIE Terror\" name and the link are interpretive, since the craft is officially nameless.",
  "interdictor->mark2": "This edge is flagged uncertain, and rightly so: it is speculation, since the only source on the TIE Bomber Mark 2 describes it as a proposed upgrade of the TIE/sa bomber itself, with no mention of the Punisher. The reasoning is still plausible as a design-history sequence. The four-podded Punisher was the endpoint of making the bomber bigger and heavier, and Tomax Bren's Mark 2 proposal - drawn from combat analysis in the Hook Nebula campaign - argued for exactly the opposite: more speed, more maneuverability, and a less prominent profile. Read that way, the arrow marks a doctrinal turn rather than a hull modification: the Empire's bomber line reacting against its own bloat, a correction that ultimately produced the Scimitar.",
  "mark2->scimitar_ab": "This link is directly documented in Legends: when Grand Admiral Thrawn rose to power, he and Sienar Fleet Systems put a modified version of Bren's unrealized Mark 2 concept into production as the Scimitar assault bomber. The realized changes were sweeping. The twin side-by-side pods vanished in favor of a single elongated fuselage with a detachable two-man command pod, adding a dedicated bombardier and an escape capability no TIE bomber had. Propulsion switched from twin ion engines to one ion engine plus interlocked repulsorlifts - costing the craft its \"TIE\" prefix but delivering Bren's demanded speed and agility, including a 1,250 km/h dive. Shields were added, and ordnance shifted to massed concussion missile racks with detonator and grenade chutes.",
  "scimitar_ab->scimitar2": "This edge is flagged uncertain, though the succession itself is stated in print: The Essential Guide to Vehicles and Vessels calls the Scimitar Mark II a next-generation model of the Scimitar assault bomber. The honest caveat - and likely the reason for the flag - is that the upgrade is barely characterized and the numbers are muddled. The only recorded changes are improved ion and repulsorlift engines raising cruise speed to 1,150 km/h and dive speed to 1,250 km/h, yet those are the same figures other sources attribute to the original Scimitar. Hull, armament, crew, and role are presumed unchanged, and no user or engagement is ever named. The arrow is sound; the substance of the change is genuinely thin.",
  "sa_bomber->shuttle": "The TIE/sh is the gentlest of the bomber's mutations: hull, wings, and cockpit stay essentially a TIE bomber's, but the portside ordnance launcher and bomb-delivery chute are stripped out for a small, well-appointed passenger pod seating up to four with about a ton of cargo. Armament collapses from lasers-plus-payload to a single low-powered laser cannon, while - unusually - a shield generator is added to protect the passengers, along with landing gear, boarding ramps, and an intercom linking the two pods. Crew remains one pilot, now doubling as gunner and chauffeur. The role inverts completely, from carpet-bombing to ferrying officers and priority cargo between ships and bases, a job it did efficiently enough despite being universally less loved than the Lambda-class.",
  "sa_bomber->boarding": "For the boarding craft, the bomber's port pod was enlarged and hollowed into a troop compartment - canon says twenty stormtroopers, two squads - and its nose gained the type's defining hardware: a retractable docking claw wrapped around a military-grade laser cutter for breaching enemy hulls. Landing gear and ramps replaced dependence on TIE docking racks. Canon actually arms it more heavily than the bomber in guns, pairing L-s1 laser cannons with H-s1 heavy laser cannons plus warhead launchers, while Legends adds a rear-facing cannon and escape-pod retrieval. Canon lore adds a wrinkle: Sienar declined to convert existing bombers, warning it would compromise structural integrity, and instead built a new craft that merely resembled one. Role: from destroying ships to capturing them.",
  "boarding->lander": "The lander is the boarding shuttle scaled up for planetary assault, and Legends sources describe it in exactly those terms. The twin-pod arrangement grew into a triple hull, and the bomber-style bent wings were enlarged and vertically elongated to carry the extra mass. Capacity jumped an order of magnitude: from a boarding party of two squads to an entire stormtrooper company delivered in one drop. The breaching claw and hull cutter of the boarding role gave way to straightforward surface deployment, making it the Empire's TIE-lineage answer to a dropship - the craft that garrisoned Mos Eisley in both continuities' background lore. Armament and performance figures were never recorded, so the change is best captured as: same DNA, one more hull, one bigger job.",
  "boarding->itt": "This arrow is speculative by nature, because its endpoint is a fan concept: a render blending TIE bomber/boarding-craft architecture with the boxy Imperial Troop Transport of Kenner-toy Legends and Star Wars Rebels canon. No official source describes such a craft, so no factual \"what changed\" exists. The imagined change is nonetheless easy to read from the artwork's logic: take the boarding craft's troop-carrying pod concept and expand it into a full rectangular personnel bay slung between TIE wings, trading hull-breaching equipment for volume, in the same way the real TIE lander scaled the boarding shuttle up for surface deployment. It is a plausible extrapolation of a genuine Imperial pattern - TIE-ifying every hull role - but it should be understood as fan art, not lore.",
  "sa_bomber->m4": "The M4 keeps the TIE bomber's skeleton and discards its purpose. Wing panels were taken directly from the TIE/sa, mounted on a similar central pod and struts - but the crewed cockpit disappeared entirely, replaced by remote-control systems slaved to a modified Beta-class ETR-3 escort transport standing off from the battle. The ordnance bay concept was inverted: instead of carrying droppable munitions, the hull itself became the munition, packed with explosives yielding five times the blast of a kamikaze TIE fighter. An oversized booster engine was added to the twin ion drives, giving acceleration exceeding a TIE Defender's. Crew went from one to zero; the role went from bomber to expendable anti-capital-ship cruise missile within Zaarin's droid-fighter research program.",
  "sa_bomber->gand": "This link is flagged uncertain, but the core relationship is actually stated in Legends: The Bacta War's Gand starfighters were heavily modified TIE bombers - the residual uncertainty is that the craft appears in one novel, unillustrated, under a conjectural name. The documented changes are extensive. The second hull's bomb-delivery system was replaced with a concussion missile launcher fed by a six-missile magazine; a hyperdrive motivator, navigation computer, and deflector shield generators - all absent from the stock bomber - were installed; and two extra laser cannons doubled fixed armament to four. Most visibly, the quadanium solar panels were cut down into TIE interceptor-style dagger shapes with a central cutout, transforming the pilot's notoriously poor peripheral vision. The result traded pure bombing for a shielded, hyperspace-capable multirole fighter.",
  "sa_bomber->lancet": "This is a loose edge because the Lancet is a crossbreed rather than a descendant, and an atmospheric repulsorcraft rather than a starfighter. From the TIE bomber it inherits the dual cylindrical pods and solar-array surfaces; from the Lambda-class shuttle family it takes the flat-bodied tri-wing configuration into which those arrays are worked. Crew grows to two - pilot and gunner - and the armament philosophy changes entirely: instead of droppable ordnance, a single powerful proton beam cannon is fixed centrally on the fore fuselage, angled for strafing ground targets, with two laser cannons for anti-air self-defense. The role narrows from orbital-and-surface bombing to dedicated air-to-ground artillery, and true to TIE lineage it kept no shields, leaving it exposed to anti-aircraft fire.",
  "sa_bomber->recon_fighter": "This link is marked uncertain because no source states the Blur was developed from the bomber - it appeared a generation later, in 40 ABY, for the Galactic Alliance. The speculation is reasonable, though: Legends explicitly notes its basic design remained similar to the earlier bomber, shuttle, and boarding variants, with two cylindrical hulls side by side between the wings and the cockpit to starboard. The interesting change is what fills the port hull. Where every ancestor devoted it to munitions or passengers, the Reconnaissance Fighter packs it with a hyperdrive, navigation computer, deflector shields, full life support, and advanced stealth countermeasures - Sienar's answer to the StealthX. Ordnance-hauling gave way entirely to infiltration and reconnaissance, while retaining traditional TIE speed and agility.",
  "phantom->clutch": "This link is ours, and it is the weakest kind the tree carries: pure visual rhyme. A Clutch is a TIE ball cockpit with three triangular surfaces set 120° apart — which is also a fair description of the Phantom's silhouette, and of the V38 and Dagger-class before it. No source connects them: the Phantom's tri-wing geometry came down a deliberate Sienar lineage stretching back to the Dagger-class, while the Clutch's fins were scrapyard improvisation by pirate mechanics, two of them doubling as landing gear.\n\nIf anything, the comparison flatters the Ugly. Tavira's pirates fitted their fighter with shields and a fin-tip ion cannon — un-TIE-like luxuries the Phantom also enjoyed — but where the Phantom concealed a 365,000-credit stygium cloaking device, the Clutch concealed pull-knob throttles that killed motion for snap turns. Treat this arrow as a resemblance worth noticing, not a lineage."
 }
};
